00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:43.433
is the perverse trade in wildlife.

263
00:14:43.433 --> 00:14:48.320
When we think of ivory, think of pangolins and their scales,

264
00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.340
think of a host of wild...

265
00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:54.440
That used to be, you think about egrets

266
00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:58.710
that were really decimated to almost the point of extinction

267
00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.470
because of their feathers,

268
00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:02.420
and other wild birds similarly,

269
00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:04.720
and some species have gone extinct

270
00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.140
because we have favored them for food

271
00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.023
or we have favored them for ornaments or whatever.

272
00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.930
But what's missing right now in this discussion

273
00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:16.313
about wildlife trade and wildlife loss,

274
00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:20.040
including a major article in...

275
00:15:21.272 --> 00:15:23.040
I don't remember whether Science or Nature,

276
00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.250
I should remember,

277
00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.460
but it was about vertebrate, the loss of vertebrates,

278
00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.263
and the trade in vertebrates,

279
00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:34.570
and the efforts that are being made to stem the loss.

280
00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:39.070
And they're talking, of course, about mammals and birds,

281
00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.713
even amphibians and reptiles,

282
00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.380
but the largest group of vertebrates

283
00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:46.053
was not even considered: fish.

284
00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.262
Fish.

285
00:15:48.262 --> 00:15:50.520
They are more diverse

286
00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:54.380
than all of the other forms of vertebrates combined.

287
00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:57.560
And yet we lump them together as, hmm, delicious seafood,

288
00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:00.750
let's go get 'em, take them on an industrial scale,

289
00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:01.920
measure them by the ton.

290
00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.050
We don't even recognize tuna as individual animals.

291
00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:10.050
We give authority through our laws to industrial fishers

292
00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.010
to take them by the ton.

293
00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.540
It takes a lot more small tuna

294
00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:17.930
than a few bigger tuna to make a ton,

295
00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:21.460
more lives lost in the process of making a ton.

296
00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:22.460
But however you measure it,

297
00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:27.330
we shouldn't be measuring wildlife as if they're products.

298
00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:28.200
But they are.

299
00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.610
Yeah, there seems to be an empathy gap

300
00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:35.850
when it comes to fish because the human mind has trouble

301
00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:39.480
connecting or finding some kind of commonality

302
00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.630
with that living being,

303
00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:43.907
but your work speaks very loudly to the contrary.

304
00:16:43.907 --> 00:16:48.553
I hear you, and I say, yes, but this is learned behavior.

305
00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.900
A child, and even me as a child,

306
00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.700
when my brother was out with a fishing pole

307
00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:56.830
and he caught this little fish,

308
00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.140
it was in a freshwater lake,

309
00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:01.053
and this fish was struggling for its life,

310
00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:04.570
and my brother had a big smile on his face

311
00:17:04.570 --> 00:17:06.220
because he caught a fish, and I looked at that fish

312
00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:09.860
and I said, "Stop! Put it back!

313
00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:12.960
It's in trouble. It's hurting."

314
00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:14.820
You know, I could just feel the pain.

315
00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:17.330
And I mean, as a kid, I didn't know

316
00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:20.620
that I should be turning off those emotions,

317
00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:22.023
turning off my empathy.

318
00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:27.160
We teach kids that it's okay to kill fish.

319
00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:30.210
We start it at really early age and celebrate

320
00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.700
whether it's sport fishing or commercial fishing,

321
00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:37.330
let alone on the large scale, industrial fishing.

322
00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:41.380
That fish are, we're taught that they're different.

323
00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:43.180
We're taught, don't worry,

324
00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:44.930
they don't feel pain the way we do.

325
00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:48.160
Yeah. We now understand they do feel pain.

326
00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:49.393
Of course, they feel pain.

327
00:17:49.393 --> 00:17:50.226
Yes, of course.

328
00:17:50.226 --> 00:17:52.610
You know, your firsthand account of watching that,

329
00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:56.050
I mean, anybody who's been fishing and has hooked a fish,

330
00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:58.010
it's pretty obvious what's going on.

331
00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:02.320
And one of the things that you boldly called for at COP26

332
00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:04.223
was an end to commercial fishing.

333
00:18:04.223 --> 00:18:08.010
Well, industrial fishing, large scale, factory trawlers,

334
00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.643
factory long liners, whatever out in the high sea is.

335
00:18:12.694 --> 00:18:16.383
And I start there because when you think about

336
00:18:16.383 --> 00:18:19.350
what it is costing all of us to have

337
00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:24.350
basically five nations disproportionately profiting,

338
00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:26.900
even though they're subsidized,

339
00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.890
it's a kind of a false kind of profit

340
00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:34.890
given the way we account for our economy.

341
00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:44.550
It's taking from all of us, if we have any interest

342
00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.913
in the state of the world and our life support system.

343
00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.150
The mark, those who buy the fish,

344
00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:57.570
whether it's for fertilizer or for food,

345
00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:02.570
for salmon or cows or picking chickens or pigs, or for us,

346
00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:07.653
these are not free goods just available to be taken.

347
00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.760
You know, we're talking million of tons,

348
00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:15.760
of wild animals that are being swept out of the ocean,

349
00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:20.313
that favor a few, and it's a cost to everyone.

350
00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:25.210
Yeah, it's horrific the more that you learn

351
00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:28.410
about this large scale industrial fishing,

352
00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:32.380
the trawlers and what they do to the bottom of the ocean

353
00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:36.830
and just the amount of catch and how much of it is discarded

354
00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:41.830
for the few alongside, you know, the shark fin trade-

355
00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.270
All of those things.

356
00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:46.030
With blue fin tuna and all of that.

357
00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:48.240
It just seems indefensible to me.

358
00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:53.240
Well, we have seen, I've this in recent times,

359
00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:57.070
increasing spotlight on industrial farming,

360
00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:01.010
where again, animals are not treated as individuals,

361
00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:03.920
really just treated as products,

362
00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:08.540
chicken farms, pig farms, cattle, whatever it is,

363
00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.130
they're just products.

364
00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.820
And sometimes you find empathy in the people

365
00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:16.550
who are working with them, but generally speaking,

366
00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:21.345
we've lost that connection with those animals

367
00:20:21.345 --> 00:20:26.250
that we consume and who they are...

368
00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:29.250
We just think about them as what they are, not who they are.

369
00:20:30.402 --> 00:20:34.590
And that somehow has changed the dynamic.

370
00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:39.590
And we think of ourselves as being lofty, humane creatures,

371
00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:45.583
different from all others in our ability to empathize

372
00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:51.960
and to somehow live in a different kind of world

373
00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.740
than the, quotes, animals.

374
00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:01.170
But the more we look, the more we find

375
00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:04.680
there's empathy with other creatures

376
00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:09.193
and often it's lacking in our own species.

377
00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:14.900
Yeah, I think that creating that level of empathy

378
00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.010
only comes through exposure and education.

379
00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:25.830
And I know that's a big piece of your advocacy

380
00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.500
is creating greater excessive ability to the oceans,

381
00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.330
getting people in submarines, getting people in scuba gear,

382
00:21:31.330 --> 00:21:34.550
because once you've had a small taste or flavor

383
00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:37.780
for the manner in which you've spent your life,

384
00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:40.960
it will allow people to connect with a greater whole

385
00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:44.300
in a way that they just don't in their daily lives.

386
00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:46.390
Well, I think most egregious,

387
00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:51.390
with respect to how we are now approaching ocean wildlife,

388
00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:56.660
is the illusion, A, that it's healthy to eat ocean wildlife,

389
00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:01.300
fish, generally, whether farmed or fresh,

390
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:03.547
there is this headline out there,

391
00:22:03.547 --> 00:22:05.698
"Eat fish. It's good for you."

392
00:22:05.698 --> 00:22:09.810
And the second one is that we to eat fish

393
00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.323
because of food security.

394
00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.580
And you hear numbers all over the place

395
00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:18.420
about how many people actually either need

396
00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:23.420
or rely on ocean life, wildlife for their,

397
00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:24.880
and then you fill in the blanks,

398
00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:27.970
is your primary source of protein,

399
00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.340
for protein, for animal protein,

400
00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:33.620
and then there are various numbers attached to these claims.

401
00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:36.120
When you peel back the layers and ask the question

402
00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.747
as a kid might, you know, do you really...

403
00:22:39.747 --> 00:22:43.543
Are we talking about need or are we talking about choice?

404
00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:47.980
Do we really need to strip the ocean of wildlife

405
00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:52.110
to feed people all over the world?

406
00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:56.440
People who have never eaten tuna before are now being told,

407
00:22:56.440 --> 00:23:01.180
we have to have industrial fleets out in the ocean

408
00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:05.713
because we need what the ocean provides as a source of food?

409
00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:11.450
When you think it through, would we say the same thing

410
00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:13.130
about wildlife on the land?

411
00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:17.690
Do we need to eat wild birds, songbird, eagles, owls?

412
00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:20.973
Do we need to eat lions and tigers and elephants?

413
00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:24.320
Some people do. They call it Bush meat.

414
00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:30.860
They really rely on wildlife in the places that they live.

415
00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:38.500
And often there's an attitude of respect for that

416
00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:43.530
and understanding that if you can work

417
00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:46.600
within a system, take some.

418
00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:50.750
But the people who are closest to nature realize,

419
00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:53.106
if they take too many, then they're out of business,

420
00:23:53.106 --> 00:23:54.503
they're out of groceries.

421
00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.830
So where you find examples of harmony

422
00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:03.320
between people who have lived a long time in a place

423
00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:08.060
taking nutrition from the wild animals and plants,

424
00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:09.310
they don't kill them all.

425
00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:15.150
When the numbers get low, either they back off

426
00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:18.333
or they lose the chance to have them in the future.

427
00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:24.170
And we have so much to learn from wisdom

428
00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:26.003
that has been acquired,

429
00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:29.470
but we no longer really face up to it.

430
00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:32.160
'Cause we've now got nearly 8 billion people to feed,

431
00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:33.170
how are we going to do that?

432
00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.390
I really get that.

433
00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:38.850
But I also understand that we don't have to feed,

434
00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:42.950
in fact, we cannot imagine feeding humans with wild animals.

435
00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:44.470
We just can't.

436
00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.320
And the idea that we can sustainably extract wildlife

437
00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.440
by the ton from ocean systems

438
00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:54.010
that have nothing in their history that has prepared them

439
00:24:54.010 --> 00:24:59.010
for humans as predators, nothing to enable them to escape

440
00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.610
the great swaths that were cutting

441
00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:06.313
through these fine tuned food webs, the nutrient cycles.

442
00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:12.337
It's amazing how we can just make assumptions

443
00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:18.430
because it's convenient to do so

444
00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:24.740
and perpetrate these claims without evidence.

445
00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:29.460
Yeah. The food security issue is an interesting one.

446
00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:34.242
I mean, certainly there are communities that rely on

447
00:25:34.242 --> 00:25:37.650
in an indigenous way, that local fair

448
00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:39.540
that the oceans and the waterways provide,

449
00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:42.960
but the percentage of those populations are very small.

450
00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:46.440
It's much more of an income security issue,

451
00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:49.500
and we need to find new sources of income

452
00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:52.400
that are not destroying the planet in this way.

453
00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:55.350
And that's not easy to solve either.

454
00:25:55.350 --> 00:26:00.160
But if we succeed in protecting the high seas,

455
00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:01.260
that's half the world,

456
00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:06.690
it's a pretty big chunk of the blue heart of the planet.

457
00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:10.250
It's oxygen generation, it's carbon capture,

458
00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:14.890
it's wildlife sanctity, if you will, safe haven

459
00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.960
with the capacity, perhaps,

460
00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:20.580
if we could at least extend protection

461
00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:23.453
for the benefit of everyone and the global commons.

462
00:26:23.453 --> 00:26:26.940
Like we have overall clean air policies

463
00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:31.940
that sometimes are recognized around the world

464
00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:34.120
as the logical thing to do.

465
00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:35.860
If we want to have clean air to breathe,

466
00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.950
these are the things you do.

467
00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:42.380
If you want to have oxygen generation and carbon capture

468
00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:45.070
on a mega scale by the natural systems

469
00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:47.880
that have been doing this now, I mean,

470
00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.410
for hundreds of millions of years,

471
00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:55.460
then we need to back off the large scale extraction

472
00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:57.050
of wildlife from the ocean,

473
00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:01.470
just as we need to stop clear cutting old growth,

474
00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.750
especially old growth forests.

475
00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:08.510
Planting a trillion trees really doesn't cut it

476
00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:12.320
if you're at the same time cutting a trillion old trees,

477
00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:15.330
got a long way to catch up with a loss

478
00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.940
of these old productive trees

479
00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.400
when you just put a little sapling in its place.

480
00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:21.510
Right.

481
00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:26.510
So, over the course of only a few decades,

482
00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:31.860
I mean, dating back to maybe 1950, in this era,

483
00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:35.560
in which the conventional wisdom was essentially

484
00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:39.570
that the ocean was an undepletable resource

485
00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:43.020
that was more resilient than anything

486
00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:43.950
that we could throw at it.

487
00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:47.120
We've now learned that that's very much not the case.

488
00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:50.930
We've lost something like 50% of our coral reefs.

489
00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:54.550
We've seen mass underwater species extinction.

490
00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:56.240
We have these algal blooms

491
00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.300
and all of the kind of disastrous implications

492
00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:02.280
of our developing world and the implications

493
00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:04.093
that that's having on our ocean.

494
00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:08.790
State the case for why ocean health is so critical.

495
00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:10.740
I mean, you mentioned carbon capture

496
00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:13.380
and how vital the oceans are in terms of that,

497
00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:16.680
but why should we care?

498
00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:21.130
Like why is this so crucial that we do everything

499
00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:25.520
in our capacity to protect and preserve our ocean system?

500
00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:28.713
When I was asked that question back in the '70s,

501
00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:35.080
by a young woman reporting for an Australian publication,

502
00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:40.310
it just flashed with me that, okay,

503
00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:41.920
so you don't care about the ocean

504
00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.713
because you don't eat fish, you don't swim,

505
00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:47.283
people don't drink salt water.

506
00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.230
If the ocean dried up tomorrow, why should you care?

507
00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:53.970
I said, okay,
(Rich laughs)

508
00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.870
you got it dry up the ocean, no ocean, what have you got?

509
00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:02.053
Only about 3% of Earth's water is not ocean.

510
00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:09.060
And that 3% is constantly being recharged by the ocean

511
00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:12.230
as water evaporates up into the clouds

512
00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:14.580
and falls back on the land and the sea.

513
00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:16.460
It's the biggest storehouse of water.

514
00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:17.930
Start with the water.

515
00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:21.550
All life needs water, ourselves very much included.

516
00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:25.330
The shorthand version of that is no blue, no green.

517
00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:27.183
No ocean, no us.

518
00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.950
So we're not gonna dry up the ocean tomorrow,

519
00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:35.963
but let's say you just modify the ocean, warm the ocean,

520
00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:40.860
make it a generator of more powerful storms

521
00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:42.970
and more frequent storms,

522
00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:46.290
make it a generator of sea level rise

523
00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:49.500
because warm water takes more space than cold water.

524
00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.610
And that's part of what we're seeing right now

525
00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:53.623
is the expansion of the ocean.

526
00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.300
Change the temperature to the ocean,

527
00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:00.850
either make it colder or warmer, and you've altered

528
00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:03.869
one of the most basic things that we take for granted,

529
00:30:03.869 --> 00:30:05.870
the range of temperature

530
00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:09.193
that is suitable for human existence.

531
00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:14.520
And if it's just water, it doesn't work either.

532
00:30:14.520 --> 00:30:18.453
It's the living ocean that makes earth habitable.

533
00:30:19.646 --> 00:30:22.010
It's taken all preceding history

534
00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:26.207
to develop this closely interacting system,

535
00:30:28.445 --> 00:30:33.020
or systems in the ocean, living systems,

536
00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:36.943
mostly built collaborations, of partnerships,

537
00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:44.333
it's like a giant symphony and every piece has a place.

538
00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:50.380
And what we have done in a remarkably short period of time

539
00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:56.653
is to derail, upend, cut swaths through,

540
00:30:57.520 --> 00:31:01.923
disrupt this amazing system

541
00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:08.180
that they say has taken 4.5 billion years

542
00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:13.180
actually to assemble, and literally about 4.5 decades

543
00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:16.430
to significantly rip apart.

544
00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:18.850
And we're doing it with our eyes open,

545
00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.813
with a smile on our face, we have laws to reinforce it,

546
00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:24.320
laws protecting shipping,

547
00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:27.140
laws protecting industrial fishing,

548
00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:32.030
laws actually giving subsidies to kill the ocean.

549
00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:41.060
Yeah. The subsidies are a huge problem,

550
00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:42.680
because they provide the underpinning

551
00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:44.510
for all of these systems that are destroying.

552
00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:47.360
I mean, one of the more heartbreaking scenes

553
00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:51.550
in "Mission Blue" is when you travel out in the Coral Sea,

554
00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:54.486
past the Great Barrier Reef and you drop in

555
00:31:54.486 --> 00:31:58.940
on what was once just an epic reef system,

556
00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:00.930
and it's completely dead.

557
00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:04.580
And just seeing how you're so far out there

558
00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:06.410
in the middle of nowhere and yet-

559
00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:07.776
The middle of everywhere,

560
00:32:07.776 --> 00:32:09.490
as I've heard one of us say.

561
00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:11.560
Exactly. It's so heartbreaking.

562
00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:16.560
And yet, you carry yourself with such conviction

563
00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:21.800
and a level of hopefulness despite all of this evidence.

564
00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:24.380
You were there many years of ago,

565
00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:26.410
you've seen this evolution.

566
00:32:26.410 --> 00:32:30.853
How do you hold onto that sense of hopefulness?

567
00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:37.490
Well, during 2020, when I had to kind of,

568
00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:41.100
like most of the rest of the world,

569
00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:45.110
sit back and reflect on questions such as this,

570
00:32:45.110 --> 00:32:47.020
you know, what reason is there to hope

571
00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:52.020
in the face of so much that's so negative, wars, poverty,

572
00:32:55.070 --> 00:33:00.070
hunger, conflict, you know, you could go on and list,

573
00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:05.840
and certainly climate, and the loss of the diversity of life

574
00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:08.663
loom large in my mind.

575
00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.840
But at that time, I was also in the midst

576
00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.642
of writing this big book for National Geographic.

577
00:33:15.642 --> 00:33:16.617
We have it right here.

578
00:33:16.617 --> 00:33:18.433
"Ocean: A Global Odyssey," right.

579
00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:24.317
And although I had thought a lot about the question,

580
00:33:24.317 --> 00:33:26.087
"What reason is there for hope?"

581
00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:32.460
I really had a chance to dive in literally

582
00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:35.600
what we now know about the ocean.

583
00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:39.967
Takes time to sift through the latest explorations,

584
00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:43.840
the latest experiments, the latest reflections,

585
00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:48.230
the latest studies on, on where we are,

586
00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:52.260
and also to reflect on what we don't know,

587
00:33:52.260 --> 00:33:53.800
which is enormous.

588
00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:56.370
You know, the more we know, the more we know we don't know.

589
00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.590
I've just been seeing that all my life.

590
00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:03.580
But we know enough right now to realize there are things

591
00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:06.130
that can be done, positive things.

592
00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:09.340
Every individual can take action

593
00:34:09.340 --> 00:34:12.140
that together magnifies into a movement,

594
00:34:12.140 --> 00:34:14.080
into a change of policy,

595
00:34:14.080 --> 00:34:17.500
into a new way of thinking about food.

596
00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:20.110
There's evidence that we have done this in the past

597
00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:24.810
about smoking, about wearing a seatbelt, about,

598
00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:28.373
you know, in overnight, when we realized

599
00:34:28.373 --> 00:34:31.670
that our lives were threatened by a virus,

600
00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:34.870
and it's not the first time, although for the first time

601
00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:38.300
we could quickly identify what it was,

602
00:34:38.300 --> 00:34:42.760
not so long ago, the existence of viruses was not known.

603
00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:44.920
We didn't even know what bacteria were

604
00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:47.880
not so long ago in human history.

605
00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:52.640
And now we have this ability based and armed with knowledge

606
00:34:52.640 --> 00:34:55.943
to turn around, and when we get it to change,

607
00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:01.274
it took us longer with seatbelts and smoking,

608
00:35:01.274 --> 00:35:03.810
and we got it with COVID-19,

609
00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:07.390
we have to get it with climate change and realize

610
00:35:07.390 --> 00:35:12.390
that by protecting nature, recovery is possible,

611
00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:16.200
and there's evidence, and that's partly what I focused on

612
00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:17.220
in my COVID year.

613
00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:19.910
Where's the evidence? What do we know?

614
00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:22.883
Okay, look at what we've done with whales.

615
00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:28.570
When I was a kid, whalers were celebrated heroic characters

616
00:35:28.570 --> 00:35:30.980
that would go out and brave the elements,

617
00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:33.940
and whales were portrayed more as monsters

618
00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:36.683
than as fellow citizens,

619
00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:40.730
caring, intelligent beings that have language.

620
00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:43.300
They have society. They have families.

621
00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:46.210
That stick together as communities,

622
00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:49.330
almost like moving cities that have liquid boundaries,

623
00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:50.490
certainly with dolphins,

624
00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:53.990
you can see that these great collections

625
00:35:53.990 --> 00:35:57.690
of several hundred thousand individuals

626
00:35:57.690 --> 00:36:00.560
that is like a city, and they stick together,

627
00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:02.060
and they move through the ocean.

628
00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.573
It's just as a moving city.

629
00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:07.493
But they communicate, they have names.

630
00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:11.960
Sperm whales have what they call a coda.

631
00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:14.640
Each one has a name.

632
00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:19.110
Orcas have for each other, for one another, names.

633
00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:22.270
Dolphins, a signature whistle

634
00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:24.670
that is learned when they first are born.

635
00:36:24.670 --> 00:36:27.300
And it sticks with them for their whole life.

636
00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:30.607
None of this was known when I was a kid.

637
00:36:30.607 --> 00:36:33.190
And as people began to know whales as something more

638
00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:38.190
than oil and meat, the attitude changed to save the whale.

639
00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:44.520
Whales movement really began on my watch.

640
00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:48.100
And by the 1980s was a voice so powerful,

641
00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:51.180
that in 1986, a moratorium

642
00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:55.010
on the commercial taking of whales was agreed upon

643
00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:57.050
by the International Whaling Commission.

644
00:36:57.050 --> 00:36:59.440
I served on the International Whaling Commission

645
00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:03.900
for four years and watched this interplay

646
00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:08.010
of different ways of looking at whales,

647
00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:11.520
which was in the '80s and '90s still evolving.

648
00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:16.520
Now there's such a really powerful, strong recognition

649
00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:18.310
of the value of whales alive,

650
00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:20.760
and I don't mean just for whale watching,

651
00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:23.840
or the value that the International Monetary Fund

652
00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:26.950
commissioned a study that was released in 2020

653
00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:30.870
at the World Economic Forum that a whale alive

654
00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:35.540
based on the carbon climate value is large enough,

655
00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:37.530
so if you take all the whales together,

656
00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:41.870
we're looking at at least a big trillion,

657
00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:46.640
big T trillion dollars worth of carbon in the whales alive

658
00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:51.690
versus a tiny fraction of that if you want whales dead.

659
00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:55.230
And then it's only once, it'll decline every year

660
00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.630
as you continue to kill them.

661
00:37:57.630 --> 00:38:01.480
So reason for hope: there're more whales today

662
00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:03.990
than there were when I was a child.

663
00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:05.700
More reason for hope because we're looking

664
00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:09.577
at other forms of life with dignity and respect.

665
00:38:09.577 --> 00:38:12.040
And it isn't just about the money.

666
00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:16.810
It's about this ethic of caring, kind of an ethic of living.

667
00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:18.540
Yeah, look at all the religions in the world

668
00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:21.310
and how valuable and important that is

669
00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:26.310
to the lives of people globally through all of our history.

670
00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:32.160
And it puts a real value on human life and our existence

671
00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:35.750
and even other forms of life

672
00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:38.440
that transcend dollars and cents.

673
00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:43.440
Huh, having said that, look at what we do with tuna.

674
00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:50.003
They have to be worth at least as much as whales for carbon.

675
00:38:51.090 --> 00:38:53.240
And we don't have yet in our minds

676
00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:56.220
the ethic of understanding or caring about them

677
00:38:56.220 --> 00:39:00.540
for their intelligence, their relationships,

678
00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:02.710
their community, their ability to navigate

679
00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:05.940
over long distances with no roadmap or lunch,

680
00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:08.290
except what they carry in their head

681
00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:10.550
and what they carry in their bodies.

682
00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:15.450
And we've disrupted the wisdom of tunas and cod

683
00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:17.650
and other migratory animals

684
00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:20.710
because we've broken those chains,

685
00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:22.840
and it's not just bad news for them.

686
00:39:22.840 --> 00:39:25.000
That means look at the carbon cycle,

687
00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:28.490
look at the nutrient cycle, look at where the nitrogen

688
00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:30.290
and phosphates and other nutrients

689
00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:34.590
that these animals spread around in a way

690
00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:38.530
that has created the planet that we now occupy,

691
00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:40.133
that is favorable to us.

692
00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:44.763
There are more sea turtles today than when I was a kid.

693
00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:50.180
In the '50s, when I was just beginning to dive

694
00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:53.940
as a young scientist, I traveled down to the Florida Keys

695
00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:57.450
to go explore the shallow water,

696
00:39:57.450 --> 00:39:59.490
the coral reef, the mangroves.

697
00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:02.580
And along the way, there are big billboards,

698
00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:05.157
I remember one place in particular called the Duck End,

699
00:40:05.157 --> 00:40:07.820
"Turtle Steak," And they had a price tag on it,

700
00:40:07.820 --> 00:40:10.360
going further to get to Key West,

701
00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:12.610
and they had turtles in pens,

702
00:40:12.610 --> 00:40:16.350
just waiting to be turned into turtle steak, in turtle soup.

703
00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:18.990
It's on my watch, there were these animals

704
00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:22.100
just being treated the way we treat most fish and shrimp

705
00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:24.620
and squid and lobsters.

706
00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:28.910
They're just things, they aren't to be treated

707
00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:31.170
with a kind of dignity and respect.

708
00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:36.170
We now do accord turtles with international agreements

709
00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:40.270
and policies and programs

710
00:40:40.270 --> 00:40:44.120
that really protect the nests of turtles,

711
00:40:44.120 --> 00:40:46.850
protect turtles on their migrations,

712
00:40:46.850 --> 00:40:51.070
laws against killing turtles, or in this country,

713
00:40:51.070 --> 00:40:54.850
even handling a young turtle that is coming out of its nest

714
00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:56.540
and making its way to the ocean.

715
00:40:56.540 --> 00:40:58.220
It's illegal to pick one up.

716
00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:00.700
I mean, we really have a different attitude,

717
00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:03.290
and it's happened on my watch.

718
00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:04.810
I've seen it happen.

719
00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:07.790
So I know it's possible to change in a way

720
00:41:07.790 --> 00:41:09.890
that will get us to a better place,

721
00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:14.890
protecting any place, giving nature a break on the land,

722
00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:20.583
national parks, wildlife reserves in the ocean,

723
00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:25.063
places that are highly or fully protected.

724
00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:28.420
And I don't include managed areas,

725
00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:32.000
because they don't really work nearly as well,

726
00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:36.720
in fact, often, not at all that what you see outside,

727
00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:39.560
a managed area and inside,

728
00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:41.780
sometimes it's hard to tell the difference,

729
00:41:41.780 --> 00:41:46.340
but where you really say, no, we need to safeguard places

730
00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:49.463
where everything is protected, everything.

731
00:41:50.830 --> 00:41:54.240
Treat these as sacred places because we don't know

732
00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:56.980
how to put them back together again once they're gone

733
00:41:56.980 --> 00:41:59.570
and we need them to restore what's been lost.

734
00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:04.500
We need them as models to be able to really imagine

735
00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:07.480
what health looks like.

736
00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:11.150
Imagine if all of the big Redwood trees had been cut

737
00:42:11.150 --> 00:42:13.830
and all we had were saplings left.

738
00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:14.950
Kids would grow up thinking

739
00:42:14.950 --> 00:42:16.890
that's what Redwood trees look like.

740
00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:19.860
These little stalks, not the giants

741
00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:23.940
that are still there because people before I came along,

742
00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:27.790
had the vision to be able to protect, safeguard

743
00:42:27.790 --> 00:42:32.790
some of these great giants against the appetite for lumber.

744
00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:35.300
We're still killing the great giants

745
00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:36.650
despite what we now know.

746
00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:39.620
But there is hope. There is hope.

747
00:42:39.620 --> 00:42:43.080
Yeah, at least we recognize that on land

748
00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:47.380
because we bear witness to it, and we've done, I guess,

749
00:42:47.380 --> 00:42:51.450
an adequate job of carving out protected land masses

750
00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:54.270
and creating national parks and the like,

751
00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:56.400
and there is a very strong argument

752
00:42:56.400 --> 00:42:58.410
that that's exactly what we should be doing

753
00:42:58.410 --> 00:43:00.270
with our oceans and our waterways.

754
00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:04.640
And that's a big piece of the Mission Blue mission

755
00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:07.020
of creating these Hope Spots, right?

756
00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.440
So talk a little bit about the Hope Spots

757
00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:11.003
and what goes into that.

758
00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:13.730
Well, I think for most of my life,

759
00:43:13.730 --> 00:43:16.920
I've been a champion of protecting wild places

760
00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:19.403
on the land and in the sea.

761
00:43:20.360 --> 00:43:22.360
I worked with the national park system.

762
00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:27.360
I love the idea that we can and do give back,

763
00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:30.091
and it's not necessarily because we see what we now see

764
00:43:30.091 --> 00:43:33.673
about our life depends on: protecting nature.

765
00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:36.820
I think during the era of Teddy Roosevelt,

766
00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:40.420
it was because these are beautiful places

767
00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:42.540
that inspire us with,

768
00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:46.630
and that basically we should protect them

769
00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:51.630
because they really are great for recreation

770
00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:57.710
and great for spiritual reasons, whatever the reason.

771
00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:02.740
But now we know that there isn't self-interest

772
00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:07.150
that really includes the aesthetic reasons,

773
00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:08.630
but really goes beyond it,

774
00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:12.383
that our existence really depends on taking care of nature.

775
00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:16.740
The carbon capturing, oxygen generating,

776
00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:19.440
planet stabilizing overall,

777
00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:21.992
the shaping the chemistry of the planet in subtle

778
00:44:21.992 --> 00:44:26.930
and sometimes not so subtle ways that favor us,

779
00:44:26.930 --> 00:44:31.930
and for us to now be at a point where we can see it

780
00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:34.730
and measure it and understand it,

781
00:44:34.730 --> 00:44:38.840
all we have to do now is to get that knowledge incorporated

782
00:44:38.840 --> 00:44:43.300
into our basic thinking and make decisions

783
00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:45.493
that really will lead us to a better place.

784
00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:48.770
So you have these, how many Hope Spots are there now?

785
00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:50.187
There are nearly 140,

786
00:44:50.187 --> 00:44:52.180
and new ones coming along all the time.

787
00:44:52.180 --> 00:44:57.180
They're different from the other approaches

788
00:44:57.220 --> 00:45:02.220
that I wholeheartedly applaud of identifying those areas

789
00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:07.940
that are still intact, the last wild places, if you will.

790
00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:10.990
National Geographic has a project

791
00:45:10.990 --> 00:45:14.280
that I have applauded from the beginning, the Pristine Sea,

792
00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:18.120
is where you identify places that are about as intact

793
00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:20.770
as they are anywhere in the ocean.

794
00:45:20.770 --> 00:45:23.510
And then you work diligently

795
00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:26.720
to try to encourage protection for them

796
00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.680
on a government level and have expeditions there

797
00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:32.710
to celebrate and share knowledge,

798
00:45:32.710 --> 00:45:35.870
which is also what we do with Mission Blue.

799
00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:39.980
We work both at the highest government levels,

800
00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:43.070
but we also work with communities on the ground.

801
00:45:43.070 --> 00:45:47.410
As other organizations, World Wildlife, Nature Conservancy,

802
00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:50.820
Conservation International, Wildlife Conservation Society,

803
00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:53.030
a lot of big organizations,

804
00:45:53.030 --> 00:45:55.210
but there are a lot of small ones too,

805
00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:58.010
that really are working at both ends

806
00:45:58.010 --> 00:46:01.200
from a bottom up, top down, as they say,

807
00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:05.850
and everything in between to try to really encourage people

808
00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:07.893
to do what they can locally,

809
00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:10.817
and with Mission Blue what we're trying to do,

810
00:46:10.817 --> 00:46:13.330
and I think we're showing some of success

811
00:46:13.330 --> 00:46:16.860
to develop a network, a network of hope

812
00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:19.580
of people who are connecting with their stories,

813
00:46:19.580 --> 00:46:21.040
with their data.

814
00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:26.040
We're working with Esri, the GIS data management enterprise

815
00:46:26.317 --> 00:46:29.410
that's now 50 years old based here

816
00:46:29.410 --> 00:46:32.080
where I am now and you are, in California,

817
00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:35.950
that has really developed the technology

818
00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:40.710
to get various kinds of information and layer them,

819
00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:43.250
And they work with cities for city planning,

820
00:46:43.250 --> 00:46:46.550
with governments to understand where's the best place

821
00:46:46.550 --> 00:46:49.130
to put a hospital given what we now know

822
00:46:49.130 --> 00:46:51.960
about the community, instead of just randomly saying,

823
00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.330
oh, here's a piece of ground, let's put it there.

824
00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:57.720
No, it's more thoughtful than that.

825
00:46:57.720 --> 00:47:02.720
So with Mission Blue and Esri, to get a story map

826
00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:06.130
to delineate, basically, here's the territory

827
00:47:06.130 --> 00:47:07.550
we're talking about.

828
00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:08.780
Here's who lives there,

829
00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:12.690
I mean, in terms of the creatures who occupy that space.

830
00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:15.670
Here are the images and we're are getting divers,

831
00:47:15.670 --> 00:47:17.920
we were working directly with PADI, for example,

832
00:47:17.920 --> 00:47:21.100
the Professional Association of Diving Instructors,

833
00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:23.030
to encourage divers everywhere,

834
00:47:23.030 --> 00:47:24.910
when they go out on a holiday

835
00:47:24.910 --> 00:47:28.810
or they go out on whatever it is, take pictures.

836
00:47:28.810 --> 00:47:31.110
And if it's in a Hope Spot, or even if it isn't,

837
00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:34.240
maybe they can upload data in a related way

838
00:47:34.240 --> 00:47:39.240
to show, "Here are my images of whale sharks."

839
00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:41.300
And somewhere else people have taken pictures

840
00:47:41.300 --> 00:47:42.590
of whale sharks.

841
00:47:42.590 --> 00:47:44.070
Somebody else has taken pictures

842
00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:46.410
of the same kinds of creatures elsewhere,

843
00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:49.580
together you really get a database

844
00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:53.530
around individual species, but also what makes a community?

845
00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:56.140
What are the fish? What are the bryozoans?

846
00:47:56.140 --> 00:47:57.990
What are the sponges? What are the corals?

847
00:47:57.990 --> 00:47:59.440
Who lives here?

848
00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:01.240
And can we see their faces?

849
00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:03.490
And can we see them over time?

850
00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:07.920
Some people are able to contribute information

851
00:48:07.920 --> 00:48:10.120
that they've gathered over years,

852
00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:12.880
even a lifetime, like Randy Wells,

853
00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:16.680
who's a dolphin scientist in Sarasota, Florida,

854
00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:19.630
who's been getting to know individual dolphins

855
00:48:19.630 --> 00:48:21.570
over 50 years.

856
00:48:21.570 --> 00:48:25.370
He's got names, he's got associations with families.

857
00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:28.690
He knows the grandparents, the parents, the kids,

858
00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:33.020
the grandkids, you know, this whole assembly of information.

859
00:48:33.020 --> 00:48:37.860
Now putting that information into the Mission Blue website

860
00:48:37.860 --> 00:48:39.790
so scientists can access,

861
00:48:39.790 --> 00:48:43.750
that makes information of that sort accessible,

862
00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:48.630
something to be celebrated and used in a more favorable way

863
00:48:48.630 --> 00:48:51.110
than has been possible here before.

864
00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:52.560
That's really beautiful.

865
00:48:52.560 --> 00:48:54.948
And the community piece is so powerful.

866
00:48:54.948 --> 00:48:59.690
It's not a situation in which you're lobbying government

867
00:48:59.690 --> 00:49:03.350
to enact a simple policy that protects these areas.

868
00:49:03.350 --> 00:49:04.700
It's really boots on the ground,

869
00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:07.350
getting everybody who lives there involved,

870
00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:09.539
integrated with this shared mission,

871
00:49:09.539 --> 00:49:12.575
and they make the decision about this.

872
00:49:12.575 --> 00:49:16.960
In the documentary, there's the test case of the community

873
00:49:16.960 --> 00:49:18.346
on the Baja Peninsula-

874
00:49:18.346 --> 00:49:19.210
Yeah, Cabo (indistinct).

875
00:49:19.210 --> 00:49:24.000
Right, they transform what was once a fishing village,

876
00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:27.560
ostensibly, into this ecotourism area and how-

877
00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:29.600
You know, we can't claim (indistinct)

878
00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:32.230
that the fishermen themselves did that,

879
00:49:32.230 --> 00:49:34.709
but we can certainly celebrate it,

880
00:49:34.709 --> 00:49:38.410
and encourage others to say, "Well, they did it.

881
00:49:38.410 --> 00:49:40.340
Maybe we can too."

882
00:49:40.340 --> 00:49:43.170
That finding other means of making a living

883
00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:48.170
is certainly more secure and safer because as they depleted,

884
00:49:48.380 --> 00:49:50.940
the local populations of fish around Cabo Pulmo,

885
00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:53.700
they had to go further and further offshore.

886
00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:56.363
And the fishermen were being lost at sea.

887
00:49:57.220 --> 00:50:00.357
And it was partly that that inspired them to say,

888
00:50:00.357 --> 00:50:04.170
"Enough already. Let's do something else."

889
00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:06.380
Right, and we've seen what has happened

890
00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:11.330
on the west coast of Africa or with Somalia and how,

891
00:50:11.330 --> 00:50:14.459
when there's no more fish, they become pirates

892
00:50:14.459 --> 00:50:16.220
in order to survive.

893
00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:17.940
So there's all these implications down the stream.

894
00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:20.000
Part of the reason their fish are gone

895
00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:23.842
are because of the high seas, or even in the coastal waters

896
00:50:23.842 --> 00:50:27.890
where European Union has allowed industrial fishing

897
00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:30.710
to come into other countries, it's not just European Union,

898
00:50:30.710 --> 00:50:35.710
but countries are giving licenses to other countries

899
00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:41.950
to come in their own exclusive economic zones.

900
00:50:41.950 --> 00:50:44.370
And it gets a little more complicated there

901
00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:49.370
than in the high seas where we're really talking about

902
00:50:49.520 --> 00:50:52.470
a small number of countries that are really doing

903
00:50:52.470 --> 00:50:56.150
most of the taking in the high seas.

904
00:50:56.150 --> 00:51:00.220
And it's also fraught with other issues of human slavery,

905
00:51:00.220 --> 00:51:02.930
where people are sure really forced into labor

906
00:51:02.930 --> 00:51:06.100
and stay at sea for sometimes years,

907
00:51:06.100 --> 00:51:10.373
where human is not even valued, let alone the fish life.

908
00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:17.240
So Ian Urbina has done a tremendous service to all of us

909
00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:23.053
by documenting the life of fishermen

910
00:51:23.053 --> 00:51:28.053
aboard these high seas industrial fleets.

911
00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:32.857
Right. Now we're having to contend with the growth

912
00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:38.460
and the rise of deep sea mining as well,

913
00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:40.400
especially as EV batteries

914
00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:42.940
and all these kind of precious minerals that we now need

915
00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:46.840
to power our ways in a manner that feels

916
00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:50.150
like it's off the T of oil and gas,

917
00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:55.150
and yet also has this tragic effect on our ocean systems.

918
00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:58.950
Right, and that's another matter

919
00:51:58.950 --> 00:52:01.140
that I really took a deep dive into

920
00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:03.943
during my year of reflection,

921
00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:09.300
but it was not the first time I began looking

922
00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:14.300
at the deep sea mining issues, going back to the 1970s,

923
00:52:15.200 --> 00:52:20.200
when it first became known that these manganese nodules

924
00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:22.760
could be commercially valuable

925
00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:27.290
and Lockheed really took an interest and began investing

926
00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:29.960
literally hundreds of millions of dollars

927
00:52:29.960 --> 00:52:31.890
to explore the potential for going

928
00:52:33.316 --> 00:52:35.910
like several miles underneath the surface of the ocean

929
00:52:35.910 --> 00:52:39.930
and deploy instruments that could scoop them up

930
00:52:39.930 --> 00:52:42.580
and return them to the surface.

931
00:52:42.580 --> 00:52:45.293
And then do, at the time, it was thought

932
00:52:45.293 --> 00:52:49.130
that at sea processing was the best way

933
00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:51.210
to deal with the tailings,

934
00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:53.570
because, hey, who cares in the ocean

935
00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:55.220
if you bring those things ashore?

936
00:52:56.140 --> 00:53:00.484
People will notice that it's a dirty business, really is,

937
00:53:00.484 --> 00:53:02.580
and could cause problems.

938
00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:06.710
But after a while, I mean, there was a hiatus.

939
00:53:06.710 --> 00:53:08.420
It wasn't just Lockheed, nation...

940
00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:12.460
Number of companies around the world really were tempted

941
00:53:12.460 --> 00:53:15.340
to think of this as the next profitable thing.

942
00:53:15.340 --> 00:53:19.550
And it was so much an issue that when the Law of the Sea

943
00:53:19.550 --> 00:53:23.840
was being negotiated in the '80s, it was the key issue

944
00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:28.700
that derailed the U.S. participation because of the idea

945
00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:33.360
that the technology that, quotes, developed countries,

946
00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:37.400
especially the U.S. have, were the only means

947
00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:41.910
that would truly make it possible to mine the deep sea,

948
00:53:41.910 --> 00:53:46.910
and that it seemed unfair for us to be able to go

949
00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:51.370
and take from the deep sea without sharing anything

950
00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:55.740
taken from the global commons in a more equitable way

951
00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:58.293
with nations that did not have the technology,

952
00:53:59.240 --> 00:54:04.140
or to have either technology transfer, which we resisted,

953
00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:08.880
or to think of how you could share the profits in some way.

954
00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:13.880
That was a show stopper, and it kind of still is.

955
00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:16.820
It's the idea that other nations

956
00:54:16.820 --> 00:54:21.820
that we would share our edge on technology and all the rest

957
00:54:23.270 --> 00:54:28.270
so that it would be shared more globally or more equitably.

958
00:54:29.560 --> 00:54:31.170
So we are not signatures...

959
00:54:31.170 --> 00:54:32.080
We'll, we're signatures,

960
00:54:32.080 --> 00:54:34.890
but we haven't ratified the Law of the Sea.

961
00:54:34.890 --> 00:54:37.610
So we're the outliers right now.

962
00:54:37.610 --> 00:54:40.070
Other nations are calling the shots

963
00:54:40.070 --> 00:54:42.060
when it comes to deep sea mining.

964
00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:45.230
We can go as observers and we certainly have influence.

965
00:54:45.230 --> 00:54:49.923
And we certainly have money to invest in these countries

966
00:54:51.120 --> 00:54:54.810
and companies that are being given leases

967
00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:56.113
to mine the deep sea.

968
00:54:57.020 --> 00:54:58.450
It's the biggest wildlife,

969
00:54:58.450 --> 00:55:01.750
biggest land grab on the planet right now,

970
00:55:01.750 --> 00:55:06.660
that is largely not even acknowledged by most people.

971
00:55:06.660 --> 00:55:09.010
You know, ask anybody in a grocery store

972
00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:11.313
or in a meeting anywhere,

973
00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:13.940
what do you think about deep sea mining?

974
00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:16.970
They say, "Huh? What?" (chuckles)

975
00:55:16.970 --> 00:55:19.630
Yeah, I mean, I admit to not knowing

976
00:55:19.630 --> 00:55:22.430
very much about it at all and was learning about it

977
00:55:22.430 --> 00:55:24.880
just in preparing to speak to you today,

978
00:55:24.880 --> 00:55:27.320
and it's rather alarming.

979
00:55:27.320 --> 00:55:32.320
But there's no doubt there's so much we don't know

980
00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:38.210
about the real nature of the processes

981
00:55:38.210 --> 00:55:43.210
that happen within these manganese nodules.

982
00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:46.620
What we do know is that they're alive.

983
00:55:46.620 --> 00:55:50.740
They're formed gradually over long periods of time

984
00:55:50.740 --> 00:55:55.740
by bacteria, microbes anyway, maybe archaea are involved,

985
00:55:57.010 --> 00:55:59.720
but we know that bacteria are

986
00:55:59.720 --> 00:56:04.720
that take the small amounts of these metals

987
00:56:05.930 --> 00:56:10.490
and other materials out of the seawater and accumulate them,

988
00:56:10.490 --> 00:56:14.000
starting with something like a sharks tooth

989
00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:17.570
or the ear bone of a whale or a bit of shell,

990
00:56:17.570 --> 00:56:21.300
something organic starts the process typically,

991
00:56:21.300 --> 00:56:24.300
and these layers are formed over years.

992
00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:26.750
So something the size of a walnut

993
00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:30.760
may have taken 10,000 years.

994
00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:32.610
Something the size of your fist,

995
00:56:32.610 --> 00:56:34.890
or they often use a potato as a size,

996
00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:36.300
potatoes come in all sizes,

997
00:56:36.300 --> 00:56:37.937
so what are you talking about here?

998
00:56:37.937 --> 00:56:40.810
But millions of years to get that big,

999
00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:42.840
and they're still growing.

1000
00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:44.470
I've seen manganese nodules

1001
00:56:44.470 --> 00:56:46.590
literally the size of a football.

1002
00:56:46.590 --> 00:56:49.150
I don't know the age of that monster,

1003
00:56:49.150 --> 00:56:51.820
but I know it took a very long time

1004
00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:56.820
to get that chunk of living rock into being.

1005
00:56:57.010 --> 00:57:02.010
So, first of all, we now are really making an effort

1006
00:57:03.950 --> 00:57:07.483
to look at old growth systems, old growth forests,

1007
00:57:09.040 --> 00:57:13.560
coral reefs are old growth that takes a long time,

1008
00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:16.510
to get all the pieces together, not just the corals,

1009
00:57:16.510 --> 00:57:19.580
but the full assemblage of small, medium,

1010
00:57:19.580 --> 00:57:21.670
and large creatures that make up a coral reef

1011
00:57:21.670 --> 00:57:23.350
or a kelp forest.

1012
00:57:23.350 --> 00:57:27.770
We might succeed in some cases in planting coral,

1013
00:57:27.770 --> 00:57:30.840
planting mangroves, planting sea grasses,

1014
00:57:30.840 --> 00:57:35.490
but just as a forest is not just about the trees,

1015
00:57:35.490 --> 00:57:37.687
it's this whole integrated system.

1016
00:57:37.687 --> 00:57:42.610
And when you take anything out, you disrupt it.

1017
00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:46.620
So keeping these places intact in the deep sea

1018
00:57:46.620 --> 00:57:51.340
until we really know what's the carbon cycle like,

1019
00:57:51.340 --> 00:57:53.610
and is there a way that we could treat

1020
00:57:53.610 --> 00:57:56.103
the deep sea creatures as a library?

1021
00:57:57.000 --> 00:58:00.040
Could we sample a few and figure out

1022
00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:05.040
how do those bacteria extract out of sea water

1023
00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:08.700
these metals, these materials?

1024
00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:10.290
Is there a way that we could encourage them

1025
00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:11.810
to do more of that?

1026
00:58:11.810 --> 00:58:16.800
Could we analyze what is it that they are doing

1027
00:58:16.800 --> 00:58:19.490
that we might be able to understand

1028
00:58:19.490 --> 00:58:21.420
and cultivate in some way?

1029
00:58:21.420 --> 00:58:24.920
Isn't that a 21st century approach,

1030
00:58:24.920 --> 00:58:28.399
not a 15th century or 16th or 17th,

1031
00:58:28.399 --> 00:58:30.660
or even the 20th century approach

1032
00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:33.770
of let's just take this living system

1033
00:58:33.770 --> 00:58:35.320
that has taken hundreds or thousands

1034
00:58:35.320 --> 00:58:40.320
or millions of years to form and commodify it,

1035
00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:44.330
whether we're talking trees or Lewis and Clark expedition,

1036
00:58:44.330 --> 00:58:48.740
let's go take the animals we can find for their skins.

1037
00:58:48.740 --> 00:58:51.520
I mean, we celebrate that as a time of exploration,

1038
00:58:51.520 --> 00:58:55.500
but the motives were much like the deep sea mining,

