I mean, I think, you mentioned saying no to the tuna

1288
01:13:13.230 --> 01:13:15.430
and the halibut, we can issue animal products.

1289
01:13:15.430 --> 01:13:17.857
We can say no to single use plastics.

1290
01:13:17.857 --> 01:13:21.120
We can choose to vote with our dollars

1291
01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:23.570
with the companies that we patronize

1292
01:13:23.570 --> 01:13:27.720
that are practicing sustainable, conscious methods.

1293
01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:29.720
But I think for a lot of people,

1294
01:13:29.720 --> 01:13:33.515
there is a sense of powerlessness or an overwhelm,

1295
01:13:33.515 --> 01:13:36.960
the existential crisis that looms, it's just so large,

1296
01:13:36.960 --> 01:13:39.006
and what could I possibly do?

1297
01:13:39.006 --> 01:13:43.653
And we hear about this term climate anxiety, so-

1298
01:13:44.590 --> 01:13:45.520
We should have climate anxiety.

1299
01:13:45.520 --> 01:13:48.750
Well, we should, of course, but we shouldn't allow that

1300
01:13:48.750 --> 01:13:50.152
to paralyze us, right?

1301
01:13:50.152 --> 01:13:53.860
So, other than the things I just mentioned

1302
01:13:53.860 --> 01:13:54.930
and that you've talked about,

1303
01:13:54.930 --> 01:13:55.970
like when someone comes to you,

1304
01:13:55.970 --> 01:13:58.717
as sure they do all the time, Sylvia, and says,

1305
01:13:58.717 --> 01:14:01.840
"I'm inspired by your example, I want to get involved,

1306
01:14:01.840 --> 01:14:03.050
but I don't know how to do it.

1307
01:14:03.050 --> 01:14:06.830
Like tell me what to do or where can I plug into this

1308
01:14:06.830 --> 01:14:10.797
where I can have the most impact in my daily life?"

1309
01:14:14.430 --> 01:14:16.280
Again, I've thought about this quite a lot.

1310
01:14:16.280 --> 01:14:21.280
And you can look around for some short answers to that.

1311
01:14:21.930 --> 01:14:23.780
There's a little book about 50 things you can do

1312
01:14:23.780 --> 01:14:25.596
to save the ocean.

1313
01:14:25.596 --> 01:14:30.050
It's a winner, illustrated by Jim Toomey,

1314
01:14:30.050 --> 01:14:34.860
the artist who does "Sherman's Lagoon."

1315
01:14:34.860 --> 01:14:37.420
The big shark is the cartoon hero.

1316
01:14:37.420 --> 01:14:39.153
David Helvarg is the author.

1317
01:14:41.290 --> 01:14:46.183
I say, nobody can tell you, whoever you are,

1318
01:14:47.900 --> 01:14:49.400
what you can do better

1319
01:14:49.400 --> 01:14:54.190
than that person you see in the mirror can tell you.

1320
01:14:54.190 --> 01:14:55.510
Look in the mirror,

1321
01:14:55.510 --> 01:14:58.663
ask yourself the question you're asking me,

1322
01:14:59.970 --> 01:15:03.550
ask who are you that person in the mirror?

1323
01:15:03.550 --> 01:15:07.300
What do you do well? Are you good with music?

1324
01:15:07.300 --> 01:15:08.940
Do you have a way with kids?

1325
01:15:08.940 --> 01:15:12.080
Are you an artist? Are you a scientist?

1326
01:15:12.080 --> 01:15:16.640
Are you..? Whatever you are, you have power.

1327
01:15:16.640 --> 01:15:20.730
And you might think that one person

1328
01:15:20.730 --> 01:15:24.210
in the midst of the nearly 8 billion of us there are,

1329
01:15:24.210 --> 01:15:27.080
is trivial and can't make a difference.

1330
01:15:27.080 --> 01:15:29.903
But when you look at history, even today,

1331
01:15:31.720 --> 01:15:34.620
there are individual who didn't expect to be leaders,

1332
01:15:34.620 --> 01:15:36.800
but they started something.

1333
01:15:36.800 --> 01:15:40.000
They took an action that they cared about,

1334
01:15:40.000 --> 01:15:44.500
and the word spread and they were the beginners,

1335
01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:46.923
beginners, but they joined with others.

1336
01:15:47.760 --> 01:15:50.990
One plus one equals two, equals four, equals, you know,

1337
01:15:50.990 --> 01:15:53.940
it can magnify, or even if it doesn't,

1338
01:15:53.940 --> 01:15:58.940
what you do based on what you know does make a difference.

1339
01:16:00.720 --> 01:16:01.553
It does.

1340
01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:05.220
And it makes a difference that you don't do something,

1341
01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:09.250
every bit as much as when you do something positive.

1342
01:16:09.250 --> 01:16:12.162
If you think, "Well, it's hopeless.

1343
01:16:12.162 --> 01:16:16.790
I'll just joy myself for the rest of life that I've got.

1344
01:16:16.790 --> 01:16:18.540
I'll let the kids figure it out for themselves

1345
01:16:18.540 --> 01:16:21.610
or somebody else will do it," you're part of the problem.

1346
01:16:21.610 --> 01:16:25.720
You are, because you're just coasting on the good-will

1347
01:16:25.720 --> 01:16:28.550
and the good behavior of others.

1348
01:16:28.550 --> 01:16:31.070
If you really wanna make a difference,

1349
01:16:31.070 --> 01:16:36.070
then listen up, look at what the problems are,

1350
01:16:36.100 --> 01:16:40.790
find a piece of that great array of things that worry you,

1351
01:16:40.790 --> 01:16:44.630
give you anxiety and say, "I can fix this.

1352
01:16:44.630 --> 01:16:46.500
I can do that.

1353
01:16:46.500 --> 01:16:50.010
I worry about the plastics that are choking the ocean."

1354
01:16:50.010 --> 01:16:52.290
So get out there and pick some of it up

1355
01:16:52.290 --> 01:16:56.220
and look at your life and determine what you generate

1356
01:16:56.220 --> 01:16:59.680
and try to do better about what you throw away,

1357
01:16:59.680 --> 01:17:00.513
and make yourself an example.

1358
01:17:00.513 --> 01:17:04.610
And you don't have to celebrate it or make a big deal of it.

1359
01:17:04.610 --> 01:17:08.910
But it's amazing, when kids see adults careless

1360
01:17:08.910 --> 01:17:12.380
and throwing things away out the window of a car,

1361
01:17:12.380 --> 01:17:13.727
they think it's okay.

1362
01:17:13.727 --> 01:17:16.280
And so they'll continue to do that.

1363
01:17:16.280 --> 01:17:18.440
You know, be an example,

1364
01:17:18.440 --> 01:17:22.053
whether it's quiet or with a megaphone,

1365
01:17:22.890 --> 01:17:26.060
try to find that thing that resonates with you

1366
01:17:27.010 --> 01:17:29.340
and go for it.

1367
01:17:29.340 --> 01:17:31.730
I see that happening all over the world

1368
01:17:31.730 --> 01:17:34.893
with kids in particular, because why?

1369
01:17:35.990 --> 01:17:39.507
They're armed with knowledge that did not

1370
01:17:39.507 --> 01:17:42.073
and even could not exist when I was a child,

1371
01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:48.320
and they're worried, or they're inspired, or both,

1372
01:17:48.350 --> 01:17:50.490
but they're not gonna to just sit back

1373
01:17:50.490 --> 01:17:53.160
and allow the world to collapse around them,

1374
01:17:53.160 --> 01:17:54.920
or maybe some will.

1375
01:17:54.920 --> 01:17:55.980
And I feel sorry for them

1376
01:17:55.980 --> 01:17:59.350
because we have to double down and do more

1377
01:17:59.350 --> 01:18:01.890
for those who aren't doing what they can.

1378
01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:06.890
If they just pick themselves up and get busy, and I...

1379
01:18:08.170 --> 01:18:12.720
You've seen societies do this in times of urgency,

1380
01:18:12.720 --> 01:18:14.180
whether it's after a storm,

1381
01:18:14.180 --> 01:18:17.630
communities come together and they work together

1382
01:18:17.630 --> 01:18:20.680
in ways they did not work together before.

1383
01:18:20.680 --> 01:18:22.893
We're doing it now with COVID-19.

1384
01:18:24.860 --> 01:18:27.603
When we're threatened with a common enemy,

1385
01:18:28.440 --> 01:18:30.653
we get together for the common cause.

1386
01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:36.680
We need to realize that the fabric of life

1387
01:18:36.910 --> 01:18:40.120
that keeps us alive is really getting shredded.

1388
01:18:40.120 --> 01:18:44.210
Whatever you can do to restore health to wild places,

1389
01:18:44.210 --> 01:18:48.630
wild things, do it in your backyard.

1390
01:18:48.630 --> 01:18:51.750
Okay, so you've got a big, beautiful lawn,

1391
01:18:51.750 --> 01:18:53.190
or even a tiny little lawn,

1392
01:18:53.190 --> 01:18:54.770
think about planting wildflowers,

1393
01:18:54.770 --> 01:18:59.160
think of dandelion as your allies, not your enemies,

1394
01:18:59.160 --> 01:19:01.960
because they are friendly to bees,

1395
01:19:01.960 --> 01:19:06.330
and bees are friendly to pollination that keeps us alive,

1396
01:19:06.330 --> 01:19:08.450
holds the world together.

1397
01:19:08.450 --> 01:19:10.460
You know, just look at the world with a fresh,

1398
01:19:10.460 --> 01:19:12.740
give the world rush look.

1399
01:19:12.740 --> 01:19:16.660
Think about looking back on the 21st century

1400
01:19:18.290 --> 01:19:21.983
from the next century, or even in 10 years,

1401
01:19:22.950 --> 01:19:26.850
what can you do between now and 2030

1402
01:19:26.850 --> 01:19:28.970
that will move us in the right direction?

1403
01:19:28.970 --> 01:19:30.723
Oh my goodness. There's so many things.

1404
01:19:33.569 --> 01:19:35.313
I have never thought of myself

1405
01:19:37.540 --> 01:19:42.320
as one who would be characterized as an activist.

1406
01:19:42.320 --> 01:19:43.690
I'm a scientist.

1407
01:19:43.690 --> 01:19:47.900
I'm a little kid with oversized curiosity.

1408
01:19:47.900 --> 01:19:50.040
I wanna know how the world works.

1409
01:19:50.040 --> 01:19:52.100
I wanna know everything about everything.

1410
01:19:52.100 --> 01:19:55.680
It's what kids start doing, and I never quite stopped.

1411
01:19:55.680 --> 01:19:58.260
And I love sharing the view,

1412
01:19:58.260 --> 01:20:00.440
which is one of the things more scientists

1413
01:20:00.440 --> 01:20:02.740
should be inclined to do.

1414
01:20:02.740 --> 01:20:05.480
And a lot of them are getting out of their shell

1415
01:20:05.480 --> 01:20:07.860
and becoming more vocal about,

1416
01:20:07.860 --> 01:20:10.640
look at this, look what I've discovered!

1417
01:20:10.640 --> 01:20:13.160
Don't you wanna go out and find something on your own

1418
01:20:13.160 --> 01:20:17.140
or share with me, the joy of putting the pieces together

1419
01:20:17.140 --> 01:20:19.970
to see a new concept?

1420
01:20:19.970 --> 01:20:24.970
That's how human civilization has been prospering.

1421
01:20:26.350 --> 01:20:30.770
And if I can have even a small part in that,

1422
01:20:30.770 --> 01:20:32.920
then, okay, call me an activist.

1423
01:20:32.920 --> 01:20:36.270
But I think of myself more as an educator,

1424
01:20:36.270 --> 01:20:39.983
as a scientist who wants to get others to see what I see.

1425
01:20:41.470 --> 01:20:45.110
It's something that began with just pure awe and wonder

1426
01:20:45.110 --> 01:20:47.610
as a very young girl, falling in love with the ocean

1427
01:20:47.610 --> 01:20:50.140
and wanting to explore and learn as much as possible.

1428
01:20:50.140 --> 01:20:54.520
You could have never imagined that the planet would tip

1429
01:20:54.520 --> 01:20:57.070
to such an extent where it would compel you

1430
01:20:57.070 --> 01:20:59.134
to have to take a certain kind of stand

1431
01:20:59.134 --> 01:21:00.749
and become this activist,

1432
01:21:00.749 --> 01:21:05.380
because, essentially, you're a botanist and an oceanographer

1433
01:21:05.380 --> 01:21:07.930
and somebody who just loves these-

1434
01:21:07.930 --> 01:21:10.690
I've been privileged, I realized to see things

1435
01:21:10.690 --> 01:21:13.270
that others have not to be able

1436
01:21:13.270 --> 01:21:18.130
to spend days, nights, weeks underwater,

1437
01:21:18.130 --> 01:21:19.420
getting to know individuals,

1438
01:21:19.420 --> 01:21:21.840
mindful that Jane Goodall spent years

1439
01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:24.710
getting to know one species very well

1440
01:21:24.710 --> 01:21:26.510
and transform the way we think about

1441
01:21:27.400 --> 01:21:30.510
not just our fellow primates and one another,

1442
01:21:30.510 --> 01:21:34.783
but really caused us to think about life generally.

1443
01:21:35.680 --> 01:21:40.130
We haven't had such a great kind of access

1444
01:21:40.130 --> 01:21:44.260
to creatures in the sea, except to some extent

1445
01:21:44.260 --> 01:21:47.500
in captive circumstances, in aquariums,

1446
01:21:47.500 --> 01:21:49.960
those who get to know fish that have lived in an aquarium

1447
01:21:49.960 --> 01:21:53.314
for 30 years, get to understand that these are creatures

1448
01:21:53.314 --> 01:21:56.380
with not like some of the creatures that we farm

1449
01:21:56.380 --> 01:21:58.730
that we take to market in a year or two,

1450
01:21:58.730 --> 01:22:00.790
that a grouper may be 30 years old,

1451
01:22:00.790 --> 01:22:02.580
or a halibut could be 80 years old

1452
01:22:02.580 --> 01:22:05.420
before it comes to your plate and you can carve it up

1453
01:22:05.420 --> 01:22:07.890
and dine on it and it's gone in 20 minutes.

1454
01:22:07.890 --> 01:22:09.543
Or tuna as similarly.

1455
01:22:10.650 --> 01:22:12.560
Takes a lot to make a tuna,

1456
01:22:12.560 --> 01:22:15.350
a lot of groceries to make these creatures.

1457
01:22:15.350 --> 01:22:18.120
But you, you don't know that when you see it at the market,

1458
01:22:18.120 --> 01:22:20.040
all wrapped up in a nice little package

1459
01:22:20.040 --> 01:22:23.610
or nicely prepared in a restaurant.

1460
01:22:23.610 --> 01:22:28.240
We need to be able to ask those questions:

1461
01:22:28.240 --> 01:22:29.600
Where did this come from?

1462
01:22:29.600 --> 01:22:34.600
How long did it take to make a lobster

1463
01:22:35.100 --> 01:22:37.870
that we dine on in a few minutes?

1464
01:22:37.870 --> 01:22:40.780
Or what part of the world did it come from?

1465
01:22:40.780 --> 01:22:44.280
What's the carbon signature in terms of transportation

1466
01:22:44.280 --> 01:22:48.123
for an Australian lobster that is winding up in Chicago?

1467
01:22:49.950 --> 01:22:52.593
It's interesting that you bring up Jane Goodall,

1468
01:22:52.593 --> 01:22:56.680
in reflecting on your life and your work and your legacy,

1469
01:22:56.680 --> 01:22:59.230
I can't help but draw the comparison to her work,

1470
01:22:59.230 --> 01:23:01.420
and there are so many parallels,

1471
01:23:01.420 --> 01:23:06.420
not the least of which is this shared sense of hopefulness.

1472
01:23:07.320 --> 01:23:09.617
Like she just has this book that just came out,

1473
01:23:09.617 --> 01:23:11.750
"The Book of Hope," which I read.

1474
01:23:11.750 --> 01:23:16.170
And I see that very kind of strain

1475
01:23:16.170 --> 01:23:18.140
flowing through everything that you do.

1476
01:23:18.140 --> 01:23:21.870
Like you have that same sensibility.

1477
01:23:21.870 --> 01:23:24.220
And then above and beyond that both being people

1478
01:23:24.220 --> 01:23:27.750
who created this bridge of empathy to the living world

1479
01:23:27.750 --> 01:23:30.960
in a way that not only broke glass ceilings,

1480
01:23:30.960 --> 01:23:34.780
but also really fascinated people all over the world.

1481
01:23:34.780 --> 01:23:36.690
Of course, Jane with primates

1482
01:23:36.690 --> 01:23:40.600
and yourself with helping us to better understand

1483
01:23:40.600 --> 01:23:44.250
the individuality of these living creatures

1484
01:23:44.250 --> 01:23:45.930
underneath this surface.

1485
01:23:45.930 --> 01:23:48.440
So talk a little bit about how that developed

1486
01:23:48.440 --> 01:23:51.930
through your various underwater explorations

1487
01:23:51.930 --> 01:23:54.030
and the TEKTITE-II, and all the things

1488
01:23:54.030 --> 01:23:56.820
that you kind of experienced in your younger years

1489
01:23:56.820 --> 01:24:00.840
that helped you realize something that prior to that

1490
01:24:00.840 --> 01:24:04.143
science hadn't really reckoned with or acknowledged.

1491
01:24:05.430 --> 01:24:09.630
Well, the parallel between Jane Goodall and myself

1492
01:24:09.630 --> 01:24:14.630
probably starts with our mothers. (chuckles)

1493
01:24:15.690 --> 01:24:20.230
Fathers too, but Jane speaks so lovingly

1494
01:24:21.400 --> 01:24:26.050
about how her mother shaped her ethic

1495
01:24:26.050 --> 01:24:29.470
and allowed her to explore as a girl

1496
01:24:29.470 --> 01:24:32.570
and later going with her to Africa

1497
01:24:32.570 --> 01:24:36.200
when she started out as a young scientist.

1498
01:24:36.200 --> 01:24:39.910
Well, my mother never had the opportunity to go with me

1499
01:24:40.760 --> 01:24:44.773
to share explorations, but she shaped the ethic early on,

1500
01:24:47.020 --> 01:24:48.830
the respect for life, all life.

1501
01:24:48.830 --> 01:24:51.610
She was the person in our neighborhood

1502
01:24:51.610 --> 01:24:56.060
to whom an injured bird or an abandoned baby squirrel,

1503
01:24:56.060 --> 01:24:58.920
any creature was brought to nurse back to health

1504
01:24:58.920 --> 01:25:00.853
and release back to the wild.

1505
01:25:01.730 --> 01:25:05.440
And I think it was that atmosphere of caring

1506
01:25:11.370 --> 01:25:15.843
that just I absorbed right as part of my being.

1507
01:25:17.010 --> 01:25:22.010
And I can't identify a specific moment,

1508
01:25:23.780 --> 01:25:27.930
but I do remember being on a beach in New Jersey,

1509
01:25:27.930 --> 01:25:32.543
a little kid, and being entrenched with horseshoe crabs,

1510
01:25:33.510 --> 01:25:35.430
these big craggy creatures

1511
01:25:35.430 --> 01:25:38.930
whose heritage started long before there were dinosaurs-

1512
01:25:38.930 --> 01:25:41.550
Yeah, back 300 million years old, right?

1513
01:25:41.550 --> 01:25:43.000
300 million years old?

1514
01:25:43.000 --> 01:25:45.860
Yeah, even more than that. But yeah.

1515
01:25:45.860 --> 01:25:48.550
And they're still with us, at least they're four species,

1516
01:25:48.550 --> 01:25:52.430
three in Asia, one, maybe some slight variations

1517
01:25:52.430 --> 01:25:55.050
on the theme up and down the Atlantic coast.

1518
01:25:55.050 --> 01:25:58.160
And I thought they were just magnificent.

1519
01:25:58.160 --> 01:26:00.410
Never occurred to me to be afraid of them

1520
01:26:00.410 --> 01:26:03.860
until adults came by and said, "Hey, kid,

1521
01:26:03.860 --> 01:26:07.070
watch out that spiky tail they're poisonous!"

1522
01:26:07.070 --> 01:26:09.480
And I just looked at them as if they were crazy.

1523
01:26:09.480 --> 01:26:10.610
'Cause I knew better.

1524
01:26:10.610 --> 01:26:13.690
I was a little kid, but I knew more than those grownups knew

1525
01:26:13.690 --> 01:26:17.470
about that beautiful animal.

1526
01:26:17.470 --> 01:26:20.540
I could imagine myself crawling on the beach.

1527
01:26:20.540 --> 01:26:23.000
And my first thought when I saw them was

1528
01:26:23.000 --> 01:26:24.040
they're out of the water.

1529
01:26:24.040 --> 01:26:25.130
They belong in the ocean.

1530
01:26:25.130 --> 01:26:27.423
I kept putting them back in the ocean,

1531
01:26:27.423 --> 01:26:30.480
and then they crawler back again, of course. (chuckling)

1532
01:26:30.480 --> 01:26:33.710
But anyway, because I was there when they come on the shore,

1533
01:26:33.710 --> 01:26:36.363
they're there to lay their eggs, which forms the basis

1534
01:26:36.363 --> 01:26:40.010
of one of the longest ecosystems on earth

1535
01:26:40.010 --> 01:26:43.960
with birds that fly from one polar region to the other,

1536
01:26:43.960 --> 01:26:46.480
but stop off along the way to get fat

1537
01:26:46.480 --> 01:26:48.870
on the eggs of horseshoe crabs.

1538
01:26:48.870 --> 01:26:52.850
Yeah, I grew up going to Rehoboth Beach and Ocean City

1539
01:26:52.850 --> 01:26:56.480
along that seaboard, and remember very well those creatures,

1540
01:26:56.480 --> 01:26:59.290
'cause for people that don't know, I mean they're very large

1541
01:26:59.290 --> 01:27:02.220
and quite striking when you see them on the beach,

1542
01:27:02.220 --> 01:27:05.160
and as a child, you feel compelled to pick it up

1543
01:27:05.160 --> 01:27:06.997
and examine it and just go,

1544
01:27:06.997 --> 01:27:09.780
"I've never seen anything like this!"

1545
01:27:09.780 --> 01:27:11.730
I mean they are dinosaurs.

1546
01:27:11.730 --> 01:27:12.580
Yes!

1547
01:27:12.580 --> 01:27:17.580
Kids are not naturally afraid of creatures

1548
01:27:17.810 --> 01:27:19.860
and they don't naturally want to kill them.

1549
01:27:19.860 --> 01:27:23.960
We teach them not only to be afraid,

1550
01:27:23.960 --> 01:27:27.840
we also teach them it's that killing is good.

1551
01:27:27.840 --> 01:27:30.840
We celebrate the killing of things.

1552
01:27:30.840 --> 01:27:35.840
And yes, my father and uncles would go out hunting

1553
01:27:35.880 --> 01:27:37.383
and bring back wild birds.

1554
01:27:39.660 --> 01:27:44.660
But I could never imagine doing that myself, and I never...

1555
01:27:50.000 --> 01:27:54.410
You know, since then, one of my uncles was a market hunter,

1556
01:27:54.410 --> 01:27:58.620
literally, used to take wild birds, ducks and geese,

1557
01:27:58.620 --> 01:28:01.490
by the truckload to market.

1558
01:28:01.490 --> 01:28:04.760
That was the way of life early in the 20th century even.

1559
01:28:04.760 --> 01:28:09.760
We don't that to wild birds in the 21st century, mostly.

1560
01:28:10.190 --> 01:28:14.670
I mean, not for commercial markets, mostly.

1561
01:28:14.670 --> 01:28:18.190
There's some exceptions, but mostly we don't.

1562
01:28:18.190 --> 01:28:21.040
But we do that, we continue to do it with ocean wildlife.

1563
01:28:22.710 --> 01:28:26.430
And that's the thing that we ought no better.

1564
01:28:26.430 --> 01:28:28.710
You can see, we can learn from the past

1565
01:28:28.710 --> 01:28:32.230
and we have to speed up learning from the past,

1566
01:28:32.230 --> 01:28:33.880
what works, what doesn't,

1567
01:28:33.880 --> 01:28:37.100
and how to find a place for ourselves

1568
01:28:37.100 --> 01:28:39.730
within the natural systems that sustain us.

1569
01:28:39.730 --> 01:28:44.730
And Jane has, I don't know

1570
01:28:46.140 --> 01:28:48.910
when he started just eating animals,

1571
01:28:48.910 --> 01:28:53.403
eating plants, not animals, but at an early age, I gather.

1572
01:28:54.400 --> 01:28:56.690
And so when people talk about food security,

1573
01:28:56.690 --> 01:28:59.853
we've gotta kill a lot of things for food security,

1574
01:29:01.520 --> 01:29:03.700
she'll look at them and I look at them and say,

1575
01:29:03.700 --> 01:29:05.123
well, look at the numbers.

1576
01:29:06.060 --> 01:29:10.290
It's actually the current part of civilization that is odd.

1577
01:29:10.290 --> 01:29:12.350
Throughout most of our history,

1578
01:29:12.350 --> 01:29:16.033
we have been sustained mostly by plants.

1579
01:29:16.970 --> 01:29:21.970
Now we have increasingly taken an animal based diet

1580
01:29:23.130 --> 01:29:26.090
or made a celebration of eating animals,

1581
01:29:26.090 --> 01:29:27.740
that this is a good thing.

1582
01:29:27.740 --> 01:29:30.320
But now that we can really use our intelligence

1583
01:29:30.320 --> 01:29:32.160
and look at the numbers,

1584
01:29:32.160 --> 01:29:35.710
going forward, we can't continue to do this.

1585
01:29:35.710 --> 01:29:37.610
At least not at the current level,

1586
01:29:37.610 --> 01:29:39.360
there aren't enough fish in the sea,

1587
01:29:39.360 --> 01:29:42.350
not enough land to support the animals,

1588
01:29:42.350 --> 01:29:45.670
to be able to even keep up with the current population,

1589
01:29:45.670 --> 01:29:49.180
let alone those going forward in the future.

1590
01:29:49.180 --> 01:29:51.750
So anyway, part of this is logic,

1591
01:29:51.750 --> 01:29:55.080
part of it is empathy for life,

1592
01:29:55.080 --> 01:30:00.080
but it's just word, at a turning point, I think,

1593
01:30:00.140 --> 01:30:03.030
in terms of how we're are going to feed ourselves

1594
01:30:03.030 --> 01:30:08.030
deliciously and nutritiously and sustainably in the future.

1595
01:30:08.290 --> 01:30:12.090
Yeah, there's exciting solutions afoot here though.

1596
01:30:12.090 --> 01:30:16.710
I think that the plant-based movement has really caught on

1597
01:30:16.710 --> 01:30:19.720
and has gone mainstream in a certain way.

1598
01:30:19.720 --> 01:30:20.770
I think it's here to stay,

1599
01:30:20.770 --> 01:30:23.690
and people are starting to wake up and realize that-

1600
01:30:23.690 --> 01:30:25.270
Just imagine if you go to banquets

1601
01:30:25.270 --> 01:30:27.890
and instead of having a vegetarian option,

1602
01:30:27.890 --> 01:30:32.890
you have a vegetarian menu with a meat option,

1603
01:30:33.080 --> 01:30:34.600
just flip it around.

1604
01:30:34.600 --> 01:30:36.500
Right. Well, it should be that way.

1605
01:30:36.500 --> 01:30:39.050
And certainly, you know, I read some news articles

1606
01:30:39.050 --> 01:30:42.890
that at COP26, there was all kinds of animal products

1607
01:30:42.890 --> 01:30:45.870
on the menu at all of these events,

1608
01:30:45.870 --> 01:30:48.520
which just seems to me to be insane

1609
01:30:48.520 --> 01:30:50.270
when you're at this climate conference

1610
01:30:50.270 --> 01:30:53.070
to address these, you know, problems.

1611
01:30:53.070 --> 01:30:56.630
Like, you know, when I hear that, it's hard for me

1612
01:30:56.630 --> 01:30:59.730
to hold onto that hopefulness when I see stuff like that,

1613
01:30:59.730 --> 01:31:03.250
that seems like such low hanging fruit and obvious,

1614
01:31:03.250 --> 01:31:06.900
symbolic, of course, but important nonetheless.

1615
01:31:06.900 --> 01:31:09.270
Well, I can look to National Geographic,

1616
01:31:09.270 --> 01:31:12.030
I think the last time they had their big annual banket,

1617
01:31:12.030 --> 01:31:15.400
it was vegetarian based when you could get some meat

1618
01:31:15.400 --> 01:31:17.160
if you wanted to, but they did

1619
01:31:17.160 --> 01:31:19.610
just what I've been suggesting here.

1620
01:31:19.610 --> 01:31:22.210
That was a while ago because we haven't had

1621
01:31:22.210 --> 01:31:27.210
big celebration recently because of the COVID crisis,

1622
01:31:27.210 --> 01:31:31.980
but there are institutions that are heading

1623
01:31:31.980 --> 01:31:33.130
in the right direction.

1624
01:31:35.830 --> 01:31:37.710
Anyway.
But I think it's,

1625
01:31:37.710 --> 01:31:39.460
yeah, to just to put a pin on that,

1626
01:31:39.460 --> 01:31:44.230
I think there is a human kind of inclination

1627
01:31:44.230 --> 01:31:47.840
to sit back and just trust that somebody's gonna innovate

1628
01:31:47.840 --> 01:31:49.570
our way out of this.

1629
01:31:49.570 --> 01:31:52.940
And I think that shirks own personal responsibility

1630
01:31:52.940 --> 01:31:57.060
and yes, there are amazing entrepreneurs and scientists

1631
01:31:57.060 --> 01:32:00.480
who are figuring out new ways of harvesting food

1632
01:32:00.480 --> 01:32:04.510
and all kinds of crazy stuff on the horizon near and far,

1633
01:32:04.510 --> 01:32:06.860
and that's fantastic, but I think it's still incumbent

1634
01:32:06.860 --> 01:32:11.030
upon all of us to do what we can in our daily lives.

1635
01:32:11.030 --> 01:32:15.840
It's one of those things that anybody can do,

1636
01:32:15.840 --> 01:32:20.800
do your part, think differently about your carbon intake,

1637
01:32:20.800 --> 01:32:21.800
where it comes from.

1638
01:32:22.760 --> 01:32:27.443
And some of those things are not really explored

1639
01:32:30.760 --> 01:32:33.380
in great detail in this book

1640
01:32:33.380 --> 01:32:36.570
where I tried to reflect on the ocean,

1641
01:32:36.570 --> 01:32:38.850
why it matters to everyone everywhere all the time.

1642
01:32:38.850 --> 01:32:43.850
But one of the things I did, you're asking about,

1643
01:32:46.930 --> 01:32:51.930
how you do explore the oceans here in the 21st century,

1644
01:32:52.470 --> 01:32:54.050
when we can go to the moon

1645
01:32:54.050 --> 01:32:55.990
and we're sending things up to Mars

1646
01:32:55.990 --> 01:32:58.390
to gather information and send it back to earth.

1647
01:32:59.407 --> 01:33:02.123
Why do we know so little about the ocean?

1648
01:33:03.159 --> 01:33:05.420
And what do we know? What are we doing?

1649
01:33:05.420 --> 01:33:07.540
So I love the fact that we're beginning

1650
01:33:07.540 --> 01:33:08.870
to put little weather stations

1651
01:33:08.870 --> 01:33:13.800
out in the ocean, monitoring stations.

1652
01:33:13.800 --> 01:33:17.430
It's baffling to me that it has taken us so long,

1653
01:33:17.430 --> 01:33:19.360
but the knowledge that we're getting back

1654
01:33:19.360 --> 01:33:23.010
about being able to measure currents in the deep sea

1655
01:33:23.010 --> 01:33:26.920
with these little devices known as Argo floats

1656
01:33:26.920 --> 01:33:28.870
and sharing information.

1657
01:33:28.870 --> 01:33:30.990
One of the ways that nations are cooperating

1658
01:33:30.990 --> 01:33:34.640
around the world is with environmental data sharing.

1659
01:33:34.640 --> 01:33:37.970
We do it with weather and now doing it with knowledge

1660
01:33:37.970 --> 01:33:40.103
about the ocean, which all ties together.

1661
01:33:41.300 --> 01:33:46.300
And to be able to imagine a time when you might be able

1662
01:33:46.730 --> 01:33:51.730
to go to Hertz or Avis and rent a submarine for the day.

1663
01:33:51.800 --> 01:33:54.210
Right.
(both chuckling)

1664
01:33:54.210 --> 01:33:55.530
I know that's your big ambition.

1665
01:33:55.530 --> 01:33:56.840
Let's talk about submarines!

1666
01:33:56.840 --> 01:34:01.030
I mean, submarines is a big part of your whole thing.

1667
01:34:01.030 --> 01:34:03.630
I mean, you actually actually have a whole company

1668
01:34:03.630 --> 01:34:05.270
that builds these submarines.

1669
01:34:05.270 --> 01:34:07.630
That's my daughter's enterprise, daughter and son-in-law.

1670
01:34:07.630 --> 01:34:08.620
But you started over.

1671
01:34:08.620 --> 01:34:10.810
Yeah, I did, but it wasn't the first time.

1672
01:34:10.810 --> 01:34:14.200
The first time I teamed up with engineers

1673
01:34:14.200 --> 01:34:19.200
was actually after I used the JIM suit,

1674
01:34:21.290 --> 01:34:26.220
this diving suit that looks like an astronaut suit,

1675
01:34:26.220 --> 01:34:27.263
or the Michelin man,

1676
01:34:28.326 --> 01:34:33.326
and went down to 400 meters off the coast of Hawaii

1677
01:34:33.590 --> 01:34:36.173
and walked around two and a half hours,

1678
01:34:37.230 --> 01:34:39.220
mindful that it was about the same length of time

1679
01:34:39.220 --> 01:34:44.220
that Buzz Aldrin and Neil Armstrong walked on the Moon,

1680
01:34:46.130 --> 01:34:50.230
but they didn't see anything live except each other.

1681
01:34:50.230 --> 01:34:55.230
I saw this amazing, just this kaleidoscope of creatures,

1682
01:34:56.823 --> 01:34:59.300
and it was dark.

1683
01:34:59.300 --> 01:35:01.463
I could just barely see up from down,

1684
01:35:02.460 --> 01:35:07.460
like the faintest gray up and deepest, dark black below.

1685
01:35:08.390 --> 01:35:12.070
But these creatures, including bamboo coral,

1686
01:35:12.070 --> 01:35:16.920
that flash with this amazing firefly kind of light,

1687
01:35:16.920 --> 01:35:19.810
well, actually like a blue light,

1688
01:35:19.810 --> 01:35:22.840
fireflies tend to be golden in their color,

1689
01:35:22.840 --> 01:35:27.840
but in the ocean, this blue fire is just so amazing.

1690
01:35:29.410 --> 01:35:32.640
And like 90% of the creatures in the deep sea,

1691
01:35:32.640 --> 01:35:34.530
or even in shallow water at night,

1692
01:35:34.530 --> 01:35:38.170
you can see bioluminescence almost everywhere.

1693
01:35:38.170 --> 01:35:41.580
Yeah, the bioluminescence thing is unbelievable.

1694
01:35:41.580 --> 01:35:44.630
I mean, that was so you're at like 1,280 feet,

1695
01:35:44.630 --> 01:35:49.050
still the deepest that any woman has ever gone, correct?

1696
01:35:49.050 --> 01:35:49.993
To this day.

1697
01:35:51.800 --> 01:35:54.930
Putting your eyes on something that no human being

1698
01:35:54.930 --> 01:35:58.470
had ever seen up close and personal ever before.

1699
01:35:58.470 --> 01:36:03.090
I mean, it was also this great age of exploration, right?

1700
01:36:03.090 --> 01:36:06.140
You talk about when you went on that initial

1701
01:36:06.140 --> 01:36:08.860
six-week expedition with the 70 men,

1702
01:36:08.860 --> 01:36:11.710
the mandate was to explore.

1703
01:36:11.710 --> 01:36:12.876
Right, exactly.

1704
01:36:12.876 --> 01:36:15.200
You're here to explore. This is what human beings do.

1705
01:36:15.200 --> 01:36:18.310
And there was that era of NASA and space exploration

1706
01:36:18.310 --> 01:36:23.260
and oceanic exploration that marked a very special time

1707
01:36:23.260 --> 01:36:26.073
that is kind of a bygone era, like we don't value that-

1708
01:36:26.073 --> 01:36:28.603
Oh, but it isn't. It really is not.

1709
01:36:32.415 --> 01:36:34.165
When you think what we have learned

1710
01:36:35.670 --> 01:36:38.640
in the past half century, which some people say

1711
01:36:38.640 --> 01:36:42.305
the great eight era of exploration was like in the 1700,

1712
01:36:42.305 --> 01:36:47.305
the 15, 16, 1700s, maybe tiptoeing into the 1800s,

1713
01:36:48.150 --> 01:36:49.760
with a Challenger expedition

1714
01:36:49.760 --> 01:36:53.980
that for the first time really circumnavigated the world

1715
01:36:53.980 --> 01:36:56.500
with a mission of exploration.

1716
01:36:56.500 --> 01:36:59.660
But when you think it isn't just the skies above

1717
01:36:59.660 --> 01:37:02.233
where our mandate is to go explore.

1718
01:37:03.120 --> 01:37:06.000
Now we know what we could not know before,

1719
01:37:06.000 --> 01:37:10.980
and every new discovery leads to new discoveries

1720
01:37:10.980 --> 01:37:14.250
in a much deeper and even more meaningful way.

1721
01:37:14.250 --> 01:37:17.010
Like the diversity of life, we once were really focused

1722
01:37:17.010 --> 01:37:20.660
on species that if you had of anything,

1723
01:37:20.660 --> 01:37:24.540
like the Endangered Species Act, well, okay, you know,

1724
01:37:24.540 --> 01:37:26.710
as long as you've got a viable population

1725
01:37:27.700 --> 01:37:30.343
and basically a small population would do,

1726
01:37:31.240 --> 01:37:33.730
well, now we understand that doesn't cut it.

1727
01:37:33.730 --> 01:37:35.940
It really doesn't.

1728
01:37:35.940 --> 01:37:38.860
Species all by themselves, and the fact

1729
01:37:38.860 --> 01:37:42.690
that the ocean has more diversity of life

1730
01:37:44.320 --> 01:37:46.420
and going beyond species,

1731
01:37:46.420 --> 01:37:49.580
looking at the main divisions of life,

1732
01:37:49.580 --> 01:37:54.540
like the 30 or so great conduits of life, kinds of life,

1733
01:37:54.540 --> 01:37:57.307
they're all out there in the ocean, in the book,

1734
01:37:57.307 --> 01:38:01.010
"The Ocean Odyssey," we celebrate that diversity

1735
01:38:01.010 --> 01:38:03.640
with something really kind of special,

1736
01:38:03.640 --> 01:38:05.410
National Geographic figured out

1737
01:38:05.410 --> 01:38:09.244
how to take a four double page what's eight pages,

1738
01:38:09.244 --> 01:38:12.660
it's four pages that you fold out both sides.

1739
01:38:12.660 --> 01:38:16.843
And there is the kind of the history of life on Earth

1740
01:38:16.843 --> 01:38:21.843
in full living color. (laughs)

1741
01:38:21.980 --> 01:38:23.900
Some of these divisions of life

1742
01:38:23.900 --> 01:38:27.620
have not previously been discovered.

1743
01:38:27.620 --> 01:38:29.560
This is here. Yeah.

1744
01:38:29.560 --> 01:38:34.000
Until my time on the earth,

1745
01:38:34.000 --> 01:38:36.500
like the whole, not only the kingdom,

1746
01:38:36.500 --> 01:38:40.720
but this great category called Archaea,

1747
01:38:40.720 --> 01:38:45.550
that now we know are associated with the manganese nodules

1748
01:38:46.680 --> 01:38:51.080
in the deep sea, they're associated with hydrothermal vents.

1749
01:38:51.080 --> 01:38:52.280
They look like bacteria,

1750
01:38:52.280 --> 01:38:55.520
but they're as different from bacteria

1751
01:38:55.520 --> 01:38:58.220
as elephants are from ferns.

1752
01:38:58.220 --> 01:38:59.963
I mean, they're really different.

1753
01:39:01.963 --> 01:39:04.913
Or I should say fungi, because it's in a different kingdom.

1754
01:39:06.370 --> 01:39:11.370
So they occur in us, in the digestive systems of cows.

1755
01:39:14.530 --> 01:39:16.250
And they're in the water column.

1756
01:39:16.250 --> 01:39:18.300
We didn't even know they existed

1757
01:39:18.300 --> 01:39:20.830
until discovered in the late 1970s

1758
01:39:20.830 --> 01:39:24.810
associated with hydrothermal vents offshore from Galapagos

1759
01:39:24.810 --> 01:39:26.810
two miles beneath the surface.

1760
01:39:26.810 --> 01:39:29.170
Now we know they're everywhere. (laughs)

1761
01:39:29.170 --> 01:39:30.550
Kind of everywhere.

1762
01:39:30.550 --> 01:39:33.810
And what else is out there?

1763
01:39:33.810 --> 01:39:36.750
The organism that generates

1764
01:39:36.750 --> 01:39:41.660
maybe as much as 20% of the oxygen in the atmosphere,

1765
01:39:41.660 --> 01:39:44.170
prochlorococcus, a nerdy word,

1766
01:39:44.170 --> 01:39:49.170
but kids can master words like poinsettia and Lollapalooza,

1767
01:39:50.360 --> 01:39:55.360
they ought to be able to articulate prochlorococcus.

1768
01:39:56.500 --> 01:39:58.710
It's fancy sounding, but anyway.

1769
01:39:58.710 --> 01:40:03.710
These are little green Bluegreen bacteria that have existed

1770
01:40:05.890 --> 01:40:09.900
almost among the earliest photosynthesizers on the planet.

1771
01:40:09.900 --> 01:40:13.420
So spanning like more than 2 billion years,

1772
01:40:13.420 --> 01:40:15.680
and they're still with us,

1773
01:40:15.680 --> 01:40:17.630
and they're doing the heavy lifting,

1774
01:40:17.630 --> 01:40:22.060
capturing carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere,

1775
01:40:22.060 --> 01:40:27.060
generating oxygen, providing the basis along with diatoms.

1776
01:40:27.460 --> 01:40:31.140
And coccolithophores, another nice name

1777
01:40:31.140 --> 01:40:34.320
that you should learn, call them coccos if you want,

1778
01:40:34.320 --> 01:40:37.251
but coccolithophores, what's wrong with that?

1779
01:40:37.251 --> 01:40:42.251
And other or organisms that are doing this amazing function

1780
01:40:45.360 --> 01:40:46.650
that keeps us alive.

1781
01:40:46.650 --> 01:40:51.173
We didn't even know prochlorococcus existed until 1986.

1782
01:40:52.140 --> 01:40:54.140
And it was on an expedition

1783
01:40:54.140 --> 01:40:56.440
in some of the clearest ocean water on the planet,

1784
01:40:56.440 --> 01:41:00.190
off Bermuda, in the Sargasso sea,

1785
01:41:00.190 --> 01:41:03.710
Penny Chisholm, an MIT scientist with her colleagues

1786
01:41:03.710 --> 01:41:07.270
used a new technique for looking at chlorophyll,

1787
01:41:07.270 --> 01:41:11.210
looking at microorganisms in the water column.

1788
01:41:11.210 --> 01:41:14.730
And they found this minute creatures,

1789
01:41:14.730 --> 01:41:19.163
it's thought to be perhaps the smallest cell.

1790
01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:23.160
it is tiny, but there are a lot of them.

1791
01:41:23.160 --> 01:41:27.020
And they're throughout the tropical and temperate waters

1792
01:41:27.020 --> 01:41:30.530
right down to the waters in polar seas,

1793
01:41:30.530 --> 01:41:32.383
and variations on the theme.

1794
01:41:32.383 --> 01:41:35.670
It's not just one kind of prochlorococcus,

1795
01:41:35.670 --> 01:41:40.670
but together this family of microbes

1796
01:41:40.730 --> 01:41:43.520
making our existence possible.

1797
01:41:43.520 --> 01:41:47.170
And who knows prochlorococcus?

1798
01:41:47.170 --> 01:41:49.100
A few crazy scientists.

1799
01:41:49.100 --> 01:41:50.550
But we need to celebrate it.

1800
01:41:50.550 --> 01:41:52.060
We celebrate it in the book.

1801
01:41:52.060 --> 01:41:54.247
We hope everybody will get on word and say,

1802
01:41:54.247 --> 01:41:57.860
"Thank you and keep the chemistry of the ocean

1803
01:41:59.040 --> 01:42:01.900
at a level that is favorable to them."

1804
01:42:01.900 --> 01:42:06.600
But as we warm the planet, as we acidify the ocean,

1805
01:42:06.600 --> 01:42:11.290
as we put toxic materials and exotic things in the ocean,

1806
01:42:11.290 --> 01:42:13.570
there'll be winners and losers.

1807
01:42:13.570 --> 01:42:16.725
And I hope prochlorococcus is among the winners,

1808
01:42:16.725 --> 01:42:21.725
because overall there are estimates

1809
01:42:21.950 --> 01:42:26.950
that the level of phytoplankton in the ocean is on decline,

1810
01:42:30.540 --> 01:42:33.983
perhaps by as much as 40% since 1950s.

1811
01:42:35.270 --> 01:42:36.830
40%.

1812
01:42:36.830 --> 01:42:40.180
So, don't despair, (chuckles)

1813
01:42:40.180 --> 01:42:43.733
realize that they're mostly still out there.

1814
01:42:43.733 --> 01:42:46.730
If we change our behavior,

1815
01:42:46.730 --> 01:42:50.650
we can shift in a direction more favorable,

1816
01:42:50.650 --> 01:42:53.650
not just to prochlorococcus perhaps,

1817
01:42:53.650 --> 01:42:55.440
but we know that we can do things

1818
01:42:55.440 --> 01:42:56.983
that are more favorable to us.

1819
01:42:58.210 --> 01:43:01.740
So the book "Ocean: A Global Odyssey,"

1820
01:43:01.740 --> 01:43:05.690
I mean, it's a remarkable and beautiful book,

1821
01:43:05.690 --> 01:43:08.610
that's very encyclopedic in its scope.

1822
01:43:08.610 --> 01:43:12.563
It captures the majesty of the ocean.

1823
01:43:13.480 --> 01:43:15.270
It addresses the problems that we have,

1824
01:43:15.270 --> 01:43:17.120
but it's really kind of a celebration.

1825
01:43:17.120 --> 01:43:18.960
And it's a sort of choose your own adventure.

1826
01:43:18.960 --> 01:43:20.680
I mean, you can open up to any page

1827
01:43:20.680 --> 01:43:23.395
and go down a rabbit hole of one particular aspect

1828
01:43:23.395 --> 01:43:25.040
of ocean life.

1829
01:43:25.040 --> 01:43:27.830
But I suppose the question is like, why this book now?

1830
01:43:27.830 --> 01:43:29.140
Like what inspired you to do,

1831
01:43:29.140 --> 01:43:31.883
you've written like 20 books over the years,

1832
01:43:31.883 --> 01:43:34.800
what is it about this project

1833
01:43:34.800 --> 01:43:37.940
that you felt compelled to express now?

1834
01:43:37.940 --> 01:43:40.970
Now the first ocean atlas

1835
01:43:40.970 --> 01:43:45.287
that I did for National Geographic was about 30 years ago.

1836
01:43:48.050 --> 01:43:50.530
And then I did another one.

1837
01:43:50.530 --> 01:43:53.293
When I did that, I said "Before the anchors dry,

1838
01:43:54.780 --> 01:43:55.780
we'll have to do another one

1839
01:43:55.780 --> 01:43:58.640
because we've learned so much so fast,"

1840
01:43:58.640 --> 01:44:03.640
and sorry, it wasn't quite that long ago,

1841
01:44:03.880 --> 01:44:05.540
but it was a while ago,

1842
01:44:05.540 --> 01:44:08.050
I can't remember the actual publication date,

1843
01:44:08.050 --> 01:44:11.240
but what I said was absolutely true

1844
01:44:11.240 --> 01:44:13.670
that we kept learning new things

1845
01:44:13.670 --> 01:44:15.550
that put the book out of date.

1846
01:44:15.550 --> 01:44:16.680
And I said, we have to do another one.

1847
01:44:16.680 --> 01:44:19.023
The next one I did was published in 2008.

1848
01:44:19.023 --> 01:44:24.010
And I said the same thing, we're learning so much so fast.

1849
01:44:24.010 --> 01:44:25.910
We're going to have to start keeping notes

1850
01:44:25.910 --> 01:44:27.400
on the next one.

1851
01:44:27.400 --> 01:44:29.720
So this book is really the distillation

1852
01:44:29.720 --> 01:44:33.430
of previous attempts to size up what we now know,

1853
01:44:33.430 --> 01:44:35.570
and to anticipate what we don't know,

1854
01:44:35.570 --> 01:44:38.440
and to encourage people to at least get up to speed

1855
01:44:38.440 --> 01:44:40.710
with what is now out there,

1856
01:44:40.710 --> 01:44:45.560
and to enjoy this greatest era of exploration

1857
01:44:45.560 --> 01:44:47.880
and do their best to keep up with it.

1858
01:44:47.880 --> 01:44:51.590
That this is like a baseline of what we now know,

1859
01:44:51.590 --> 01:44:54.300
something we can look back on in 10 years, like 2030,

1860
01:44:54.300 --> 01:44:57.990
and say, all right, how did we do? Where are we?

1861
01:44:57.990 --> 01:45:02.360
Here's what we knew back in 2021.

1862
01:45:02.360 --> 01:45:07.360
And there was a call in at that time, the next 10 years,

1863
01:45:07.810 --> 01:45:11.260
the most important, the next 10,000 years.

1864
01:45:11.260 --> 01:45:14.670
This mapping endeavor, this era of exploration

1865
01:45:14.670 --> 01:45:16.300
that now nations are coming together,

1866
01:45:16.300 --> 01:45:21.300
the decade of ocean research, we're now just embarking

1867
01:45:22.180 --> 01:45:26.880
on this concerted effort to answer these questions

1868
01:45:26.880 --> 01:45:29.160
and put things in perspective.

1869
01:45:29.160 --> 01:45:33.360
And I did not undertake this all by myself,

1870
01:45:33.360 --> 01:45:36.850
you know, had a team of people working

1871
01:45:36.850 --> 01:45:41.087
at National Geographic to gather information.

1872
01:45:41.087 --> 01:45:44.220
"Had you seen this?" or "Had you checked out that?"

1873
01:45:44.220 --> 01:45:46.830
or "How about this?" or "Had you talked with so and so?"

1874
01:45:46.830 --> 01:45:51.470
And their voices of those others are in there,

1875
01:45:51.470 --> 01:45:55.580
and several dozen visionaries, champions

1876
01:45:55.580 --> 01:45:58.360
who are not only looking at the state of knowledge

1877
01:45:58.360 --> 01:46:01.550
and are enhancing it with their work,

1878
01:46:01.550 --> 01:46:06.480
but are also a number of the individuals, the heroes

1879
01:46:06.480 --> 01:46:09.410
that are celebrated there, are doing something about it,

1880
01:46:09.410 --> 01:46:12.573
trying to safeguard the ocean, safeguard the earth,

1881
01:46:13.530 --> 01:46:16.610
armed with unprecedented knowledge,

1882
01:46:16.610 --> 01:46:20.910
but also understanding the precautionary principle,

1883
01:46:20.910 --> 01:46:25.910
given the unknowns, why would we dare exploit places

1884
01:46:26.530 --> 01:46:30.810
that we have not yet explored like the deep sea?

1885
01:46:30.810 --> 01:46:34.600
How can we possibly in good conscience

1886
01:46:34.600 --> 01:46:38.120
go out in the high seas and just rip holes

1887
01:46:38.120 --> 01:46:42.340
through those tightly knit communities of life

1888
01:46:43.320 --> 01:46:46.260
because we can, just because we can,

1889
01:46:46.260 --> 01:46:48.584
and because they're subsidized, we can,

1890
01:46:48.584 --> 01:46:50.770
and there's a market for it, we can?

1891
01:46:50.770 --> 01:46:54.517
No, we should look ourselves in the mirror and say,

1892
01:46:54.517 --> 01:46:56.760
we've gotta stop this.

1893
01:46:56.760 --> 01:47:00.440
We have to use whatever power of the marketplace of choice,

1894
01:47:00.440 --> 01:47:05.440
of influence to just do everything we can

1895
01:47:06.920 --> 01:47:11.920
to embrace the remaining natural wild places, land and sea,

1896
01:47:13.210 --> 01:47:18.210
and to safeguard them early and understand why we need them

1897
01:47:18.283 --> 01:47:23.283
and they need us to really reverse the trend of decline,

1898
01:47:24.210 --> 01:47:25.680
while we still have time.

1899
01:47:25.680 --> 01:47:30.580
What percentage of the ocean is currently

1900
01:47:30.580 --> 01:47:32.720
under some level of protection?

1901
01:47:32.720 --> 01:47:33.574
It's very small, I know.

1902
01:47:33.574 --> 01:47:34.407
(indistinct) about 15%.

1903
01:47:34.407 --> 01:47:37.058
15? That's actually higher than I thought.

1904
01:47:37.058 --> 01:47:38.070
No.

1905
01:47:38.070 --> 01:47:42.133
Well, it's beginning to scale up.

1906
01:47:43.220 --> 01:47:46.910
Not long ago, it was maybe 12%.

1907
01:47:46.910 --> 01:47:48.340
So we're getting a little bit better,

1908
01:47:48.340 --> 01:47:52.334
but we need to do it scaling up and speeding up.

1909
01:47:52.334 --> 01:47:56.374
Identify places here in California, in your backyard,

1910
01:47:56.374 --> 01:47:59.230
you can make your backyard better.

1911
01:47:59.230 --> 01:48:01.240
Even if you're already doing a pretty good job

1912
01:48:01.240 --> 01:48:03.890
of planting wildflowers and wild plants

1913
01:48:03.890 --> 01:48:08.890
and seeing how much of the land in your community,

1914
01:48:10.480 --> 01:48:13.060
in your city needs to be paved over.

1915
01:48:13.060 --> 01:48:16.550
Can we release rivers?

1916
01:48:16.550 --> 01:48:18.900
There's a move now to, I dunno what to call it,

1917
01:48:19.835 --> 01:48:22.570
daylighting rivers that are now in culverts

1918
01:48:23.520 --> 01:48:27.930
to be a better source of absorbing rain when it falls,

1919
01:48:27.930 --> 01:48:30.780
instead of letting it run off and causing problems

1920
01:48:30.780 --> 01:48:32.840
in the ocean and flooding

1921
01:48:32.840 --> 01:48:35.900
that is harming our infrastructure.

1922
01:48:35.900 --> 01:48:40.900
Things that are now armed with these new technologies,

1923
01:48:40.910 --> 01:48:44.850
GIS technologies, we can look high in the sky

1924
01:48:44.850 --> 01:48:48.630
and bore down into the ground itself

1925
01:48:48.630 --> 01:48:51.860
and find out what are the actions we can take

1926
01:48:51.860 --> 01:48:54.100
that for me personally, and for my community,

1927
01:48:54.100 --> 01:48:58.548
for my country, for the world will make it a better place?

1928
01:48:58.548 --> 01:48:59.810
That we can, you know...

1929
01:48:59.810 --> 01:49:02.700
Individuals are stepping up and planting milkweed,

1930
01:49:02.700 --> 01:49:07.700
so the migrating Monarch butterflies

1931
01:49:08.000 --> 01:49:11.563
can have their sustenance back.

1932
01:49:12.620 --> 01:49:15.360
We've taken away their breakfast, lunch, and dinner,

1933
01:49:15.360 --> 01:49:19.700
and their numbers have been dropping precipitously

1934
01:49:19.700 --> 01:49:21.573
as our numbers have increased.

1935
01:49:22.740 --> 01:49:27.740
And if we want to have a world with Monarchs and birds

1936
01:49:28.320 --> 01:49:33.320
and coral reefs and the things that we aesthetic value,

1937
01:49:33.900 --> 01:49:35.410
we know what to do.

1938
01:49:35.410 --> 01:49:37.897
We need to make life better for them.

1939
01:49:37.897 --> 01:49:40.480
And we can do it one backyard

1940
01:49:40.480 --> 01:49:42.870
or one community garden at a time,

1941
01:49:42.870 --> 01:49:45.671
but we don't have time to waste.

1942
01:49:45.671 --> 01:49:46.504
No.

1943
01:49:46.504 --> 01:49:49.640
You you've mentioned a couple times, this 10 year window,

1944
01:49:49.640 --> 01:49:52.420
That is really this crucial period of time

1945
01:49:52.420 --> 01:49:56.830
in which we have to act and start to, or, you know,

1946
01:49:56.830 --> 01:49:58.700
really turn this thing around.

1947
01:49:58.700 --> 01:50:01.800
You also have this initiative, the 30 by 30 initiative,

1948
01:50:01.800 --> 01:50:05.190
so by 2030 to protect 30%.

1949
01:50:05.190 --> 01:50:06.650
So, tell me about that.

1950
01:50:06.650 --> 01:50:09.670
To further respond to your question,

1951
01:50:09.670 --> 01:50:12.880
the real number for ocean protection is about 3%,

1952
01:50:12.880 --> 01:50:14.343
a little less than 3%.

1953
01:50:15.670 --> 01:50:20.540
And that means 97% is open for fishing of various sorts,

1954
01:50:20.540 --> 01:50:23.040
including this half the world open

1955
01:50:23.040 --> 01:50:27.033
for large scale industrial fishing, half of the world.

1956
01:50:28.090 --> 01:50:32.430
That's 60% of the ocean. Well, of the high seas.

1957
01:50:32.430 --> 01:50:35.350
And then within national waters altogether,

1958
01:50:35.350 --> 01:50:37.636
97% is open for taking.

1959
01:50:37.636 --> 01:50:42.636
And drilling is done in a large portion of this.

1960
01:50:44.570 --> 01:50:47.170
Obviously, there are actions now being taken,

1961
01:50:47.170 --> 01:50:51.430
but mining, opening up the deep sea and the high seas

1962
01:50:51.430 --> 01:50:54.160
or in coastal waters for mining

1963
01:50:54.160 --> 01:50:57.970
is such a mistake at this point in time,

1964
01:50:57.970 --> 01:50:59.920
when we see decline all around us

1965
01:51:01.260 --> 01:51:04.210
and continuing the large scale extraction of wildlife.

1966
01:51:04.210 --> 01:51:07.455
It's perverse. We need to protect wildlife.

1967
01:51:07.455 --> 01:51:10.577
We gotta protect the birds, gotta protect the trees.

1968
01:51:10.577 --> 01:51:12.940
But what about protecting the fish

1969
01:51:12.940 --> 01:51:14.290
instead of eating them all?

1970
01:51:15.660 --> 01:51:19.510
If you have to, if it's your sustenance, okay.

1971
01:51:19.510 --> 01:51:23.850
But if you're just taking them, because it's fun,

1972
01:51:23.850 --> 01:51:26.080
the joy of killing something,

1973
01:51:26.080 --> 01:51:30.950
or because you're using fish for money,

1974
01:51:30.950 --> 01:51:34.490
and again, I take it that there are coastal communities

1975
01:51:34.490 --> 01:51:37.080
and island countries that have this habit

1976
01:51:37.970 --> 01:51:41.340
of cutting their trees to sell for lumber.

1977
01:51:41.340 --> 01:51:44.760
And we're beginning to see alarm

1978
01:51:44.760 --> 01:51:46.617
and people coming, stepping up and say,

1979
01:51:46.617 --> 01:51:48.990
"I'm gonna pay you not to kill the trees,"

1980
01:51:48.990 --> 01:51:51.693
because everybody benefits from having the trees.

1981
01:51:52.590 --> 01:51:56.067
So far, there hasn't been much in the way of those who say,

1982
01:51:56.067 --> 01:51:57.690
"Stop killing the fish."

1983
01:51:57.690 --> 01:51:59.770
Everybody needs the fish, all of us benefit

1984
01:51:59.770 --> 01:52:02.260
from having them capturing the carbon,

1985
01:52:02.260 --> 01:52:06.380
maintaining wild systems.

1986
01:52:06.380 --> 01:52:10.350
The fabric of life is at risk because of what we're doing

1987
01:52:10.350 --> 01:52:13.630
on a industrial scale to ocean wildlife.

1988
01:52:13.630 --> 01:52:17.100
Give them a break. No, it's beginning.

1989
01:52:17.100 --> 01:52:21.370
But I feel sometimes as if I'm regarded as a crazy person,

1990
01:52:21.370 --> 01:52:22.387
because I say such things,

1991
01:52:22.387 --> 01:52:25.340
it's just because I see it so clearly.

1992
01:52:25.340 --> 01:52:28.090
I can't not share the view.

1993
01:52:28.090 --> 01:52:28.923
Yeah.

1994
01:52:28.923 --> 01:52:30.150
Well, I think the world

1995
01:52:30.150 --> 01:52:33.580
is catching up to you, Sylvia, finally.

1996
01:52:33.580 --> 01:52:36.753
I'm hopeful about that. I know you are.

1997
01:52:38.872 --> 01:52:42.770
You're so vital, you have such good energy

1998
01:52:42.770 --> 01:52:45.350
and so much of it, and at your age,

1999
01:52:45.350 --> 01:52:50.350
like how do you keep that battery powered?

2000
01:52:50.410 --> 01:52:54.580
You know, people are discriminated against one another

2001
01:52:54.580 --> 01:52:55.650
for various reasons.

2002
01:52:55.650 --> 01:52:57.060
You're too tall. You're too short.

2003
01:52:57.060 --> 01:52:58.260
You're too fat. You're too thin.

2004
01:52:58.260 --> 01:53:00.940
You're this color. You're that color.

2005
01:53:00.940 --> 01:53:02.523
Ageism is another one.

2006
01:53:03.450 --> 01:53:05.690
People look at me and they say, "You're too old."

2007
01:53:05.690 --> 01:53:07.440
Used to be, "You're too young, you can't do that."

2008
01:53:07.440 --> 01:53:09.780
Or "You're a girl, you can't do that."

2009
01:53:09.780 --> 01:53:13.370
I say, it's mostly what you think you can do.

2010
01:53:13.370 --> 01:53:17.020
Of course, there are limitations that if you're sick

2011
01:53:17.020 --> 01:53:21.233
or you have problems with walking or whatever, but.

2012
01:53:26.580 --> 01:53:29.730
you should be allowed to define what your limits are

2013
01:53:29.730 --> 01:53:30.990
instead of having them defined for you.

2014
01:53:30.990 --> 01:53:31.823
A hundred percent!

2015
01:53:31.823 --> 01:53:35.440
I mean, I think a big piece of it, if I could be so bold,

2016
01:53:35.440 --> 01:53:39.440
and this is projecting, is that you have this thing

2017
01:53:39.440 --> 01:53:40.780
that you feel so strongly about,

2018
01:53:40.780 --> 01:53:42.310
that you're so passionate about,

2019
01:53:42.310 --> 01:53:44.400
it's work that has no end.

2020
01:53:44.400 --> 01:53:48.370
There is a sense of responsibility and a calling

2021
01:53:48.370 --> 01:53:50.633
in service to something greater than yourself.

2022
01:53:50.633 --> 01:53:54.060
And I think there's something about that equation

2023
01:53:54.060 --> 01:53:58.310
and the people that I know that kind of live in that space

2024
01:53:58.310 --> 01:54:00.942
seem to be, you know, much more vital

2025
01:54:00.942 --> 01:54:02.550
through their later years

2026
01:54:02.550 --> 01:54:04.880
than other people that I know in my life.

2027
01:54:04.880 --> 01:54:09.863
Yeah, I can't stand the thought that people feel bored.

2028
01:54:11.210 --> 01:54:14.160
I wanna say to everyone who says "I'm bored."

2029
01:54:14.160 --> 01:54:17.510
Okay, I'll take your time. I'd love to have your time.

2030
01:54:17.510 --> 01:54:20.130
I'll use it.
(Rich laughs)

2031
01:54:20.130 --> 01:54:23.340
Give it to me! You don't know what to do with yourself.

2032
01:54:23.340 --> 01:54:28.160
There's so many ways to go, the books I yet read,

2033
01:54:28.160 --> 01:54:32.400
they're sitting there taunting me, "read me, read me!"

2034
01:54:32.400 --> 01:54:35.170
Places I haven't been, "Come see me!"

2035
01:54:35.170 --> 01:54:36.510
Where do you want to go?

2036
01:54:36.510 --> 01:54:38.190
What is ne what's on the horizon?

2037
01:54:38.190 --> 01:54:40.983
Or what gets you excited in terms of projects

2038
01:54:40.983 --> 01:54:42.007
for the future?

2039
01:54:42.007 --> 01:54:46.270
Well, I haven't seen all of the Hope Spots personally yet.

2040
01:54:46.270 --> 01:54:50.210
The deep sea beckons always, everywhere,

2041
01:54:50.210 --> 01:54:51.703
even in places that you think you know,

2042
01:54:51.703 --> 01:54:54.490
like here along the coast of California,

2043
01:54:54.490 --> 01:54:57.960
who has been a thousand feet down to see who lives there,

2044
01:54:57.960 --> 01:55:02.960
and make the relationship to what is going on above.

2045
01:55:03.540 --> 01:55:07.450
During the National Geographic, Goldman Foundation,

2046
01:55:07.450 --> 01:55:11.393
NOAA project called Sustainable Seas for five years,

2047
01:55:12.300 --> 01:55:17.300
we actually use little submersibles to go on ships

2048
01:55:18.290 --> 01:55:21.070
and visit the national marine sanctuaries,

2049
01:55:21.070 --> 01:55:26.070
that, quite frankly, don't protect life that's there.

2050
01:55:26.700 --> 01:55:29.770
You can fish squid off Monterey, for example,

2051
01:55:29.770 --> 01:55:32.060
and sport fishing is encouraged

2052
01:55:32.060 --> 01:55:33.500
in most of the marine sanctuary.

2053
01:55:33.500 --> 01:55:35.850
So I consider them managed areas,

2054
01:55:35.850 --> 01:55:38.470
helpful, but not all that useful

2055
01:55:38.470 --> 01:55:40.730
in terms of real protection.

2056
01:55:40.730 --> 01:55:45.730
But, okay, so in Monterey, we had a teacher

2057
01:55:47.120 --> 01:55:50.300
who joined the project, learned how to drive

2058
01:55:50.300 --> 01:55:53.870
these little one person subs and got his kids,

2059
01:55:53.870 --> 01:55:57.810
his high school kids to learn to dive during the year

2060
01:55:57.810 --> 01:56:01.533
that we really focused on the California coast.

2061
01:56:02.750 --> 01:56:06.900
There was a period when our whole expedition

2062
01:56:06.900 --> 01:56:09.220
landed in Monterey.

2063
01:56:09.220 --> 01:56:14.220
The kids had been studying methods of assessing the nature

2064
01:56:15.440 --> 01:56:19.870
of life down to 80 feet using scuba, doing transect studies,

2065
01:56:19.870 --> 01:56:22.350
counting the fish, looking at the animals,

2066
01:56:22.350 --> 01:56:25.130
relating it to the kelp forest and the sea otters

2067
01:56:25.130 --> 01:56:26.130
and the birds above.

2068
01:56:26.990 --> 01:56:31.990
And then their teacher came along as a submarine pilot

2069
01:56:32.390 --> 01:56:37.193
and went down not to 80 feet, he went down to 800 feet.

2070
01:56:37.193 --> 01:56:42.193
And what he saw: no kelp, no sea otrers, no abalone.

2071
01:56:42.510 --> 01:56:47.510
The place was owned by brittle stars and basket stars

2072
01:56:49.850 --> 01:56:53.630
and creatures like little shrimp

2073
01:56:53.630 --> 01:56:56.960
that were spaced a certain distance apart.

2074
01:56:56.960 --> 01:56:59.760
They looked like cars parked on a parking lot.

2075
01:56:59.760 --> 01:57:03.850
They were just all lined up on a muddy bottom

2076
01:57:03.850 --> 01:57:07.930
that underneath that mud was not just, you know, yuck

2077
01:57:07.930 --> 01:57:10.420
filled with creatures like another city

2078
01:57:10.420 --> 01:57:13.350
beneath the bottom of the ocean.

2079
01:57:13.350 --> 01:57:16.163
And it was like, this is Monterey,

2080
01:57:17.260 --> 01:57:20.292
as well as this is Monterey.

2081
01:57:20.292 --> 01:57:23.280
And we didn't get down to 8,000 feet,

2082
01:57:23.280 --> 01:57:25.480
but that would be another version

2083
01:57:25.480 --> 01:57:28.290
of what we think we know is Monterey.

2084
01:57:28.290 --> 01:57:33.165
So the opportunity that I really want to seize

2085
01:57:33.165 --> 01:57:36.720
and take advantage of this point in time

2086
01:57:36.720 --> 01:57:40.400
is to go deeper, stay longer, really get to know

2087
01:57:41.710 --> 01:57:44.570
where most of life on earth actually lives

2088
01:57:44.570 --> 01:57:47.740
in the dark, below where divers can go.

2089
01:57:47.740 --> 01:57:49.660
And I want others to go too.

2090
01:57:49.660 --> 01:57:52.600
I want to democratize access to the sea

2091
01:57:52.600 --> 01:57:57.600
and be around long enough to see my grandkids out there

2092
01:57:58.070 --> 01:58:03.070
just enjoying the view, if you will, and kids everywhere.

2093
01:58:03.400 --> 01:58:06.490
This is a time when I think we're going to begin

2094
01:58:06.490 --> 01:58:10.790
to see what life on earth is really about.

2095
01:58:10.790 --> 01:58:13.120
As we gain access to the...

2096
01:58:13.120 --> 01:58:16.130
Hey, when I was a child, there used to be talk about,

2097
01:58:16.130 --> 01:58:18.500
hey, maybe the moon is made of green cheese, you know,

2098
01:58:18.500 --> 01:58:19.580
let's go find out.

2099
01:58:19.580 --> 01:58:21.780
But the idea of actually going to the moon

2100
01:58:22.800 --> 01:58:26.170
when I was 10 years old, that was so unrealistic.

2101
01:58:26.170 --> 01:58:30.370
The thought now of going down to have kids explore the ocean

2102
01:58:30.370 --> 01:58:31.943
seems so unrealistic.

2103
01:58:32.830 --> 01:58:35.800
The idea of people making choices, that don't include

2104
01:58:35.800 --> 01:58:39.930
what we lump together of seafood seems so unrealistic,

2105
01:58:39.930 --> 01:58:44.770
but you know, if you don't aim for something,

2106
01:58:44.770 --> 01:58:46.780
you'll never get to that place,

2107
01:58:46.780 --> 01:58:48.130
you might stumble on it somehow,

2108
01:58:48.130 --> 01:58:52.740
but we need to get to a better place by conscious action.

2109
01:58:52.740 --> 01:58:54.350
We know what to do.

2110
01:58:54.350 --> 01:58:58.030
And so I'm ready to go.

2111
01:58:58.030 --> 01:59:00.800
As long as I can breathe, I'm ready to go.

2112
01:59:00.800 --> 01:59:02.660
Well, I can't think of a better way

2113
01:59:02.660 --> 01:59:05.900
to land this enterprise, this conversation

2114
01:59:05.900 --> 01:59:07.680
than what you just shared with me.

2115
01:59:07.680 --> 01:59:08.513
Thank you for that.

2116
01:59:08.513 --> 01:59:11.360
It's so eloquently and beautifully stated.

2117
01:59:11.360 --> 01:59:16.360
And that is an aspiration that I think we can all inhabit

2118
01:59:17.040 --> 01:59:18.170
in our own way.

2119
01:59:18.170 --> 01:59:20.380
So I appreciate you so much

2120
01:59:20.380 --> 01:59:25.380
and aspire to your level of hopefulness and activism.

2121
01:59:25.830 --> 01:59:28.030
And if there's anything I can do to support you,

2122
01:59:28.030 --> 01:59:31.170
I just really appreciate you coming here today.

2123
01:59:31.170 --> 01:59:35.100
One thing maybe, a last thing is just to,

2124
01:59:35.100 --> 01:59:36.850
while you're looking in the mirror,

2125
01:59:38.270 --> 01:59:42.933
just imagine that you didn't have the capacity to do that.

2126
01:59:44.356 --> 01:59:49.356
Even if you can't see to evaluate the gift of life,

2127
01:59:52.110 --> 01:59:57.110
imagine not having that, and why not savor every moment

2128
01:59:57.980 --> 01:59:59.673
and value it, enjoy it,

2129
02:00:01.103 --> 02:00:04.693
and when you're starting to feel doom and gloom,

2130
02:00:05.900 --> 02:00:10.900
just realize that you have the power

2131
02:00:11.870 --> 02:00:16.130
that no previous people on earth had,

2132
02:00:16.130 --> 02:00:19.060
that's the power of understanding who we are,

2133
02:00:19.060 --> 02:00:21.920
where we've come from, where we might go,

2134
02:00:21.920 --> 02:00:23.910
the power of knowing that superpower,

2135
02:00:23.910 --> 02:00:28.910
I think of it that way, and get up and get going.

2136
02:00:31.094 --> 02:00:35.050
And don't think that it's all over.

2137
02:00:35.050 --> 02:00:37.257
It isn't until it's over and it's not over.

2138
02:00:37.257 --> 02:00:41.680
And what you do can empower the next generation

2139
02:00:41.680 --> 02:00:44.080
just as what we have now has been empowered

2140
02:00:44.080 --> 02:00:49.030
by the previous one ones, plural, through all time.

2141
02:00:49.030 --> 02:00:52.200
We are right at the cutting edge of the latest and greatest

2142
02:00:53.120 --> 02:00:57.253
of what all humans have been able to figure out and deliver.

2143
02:00:58.090 --> 02:01:01.723
And here we are, lucky us, to savor it.

2144
02:01:02.710 --> 02:01:06.620
Lucky us. It's not over.

2145
02:01:06.620 --> 02:01:09.000
But it is an important time

2146
02:01:09.000 --> 02:01:10.530
and we have to take it seriously.

2147
02:01:10.530 --> 02:01:13.590
And we need to be more conscious about our daily actions

2148
02:01:13.590 --> 02:01:15.600
and our behaviors, but those are things

2149
02:01:15.600 --> 02:01:18.990
that we're all capable of doing.

2150
02:01:18.990 --> 02:01:20.930
And we're all empowered to be able

2151
02:01:20.930 --> 02:01:22.700
to make positive changes in our lives.

2152
02:01:22.700 --> 02:01:27.210
So thank you for setting the tone and blazing the path.

2153
02:01:27.210 --> 02:01:29.017
Sylvia, I appreciate you.

2154
02:01:29.017 --> 02:01:31.540
The book is beautiful.

2155
02:01:31.540 --> 02:01:34.830
It's called "Ocean: A Global Odyssey."

2156
02:01:34.830 --> 02:01:39.610
It is a book that is packed

2157
02:01:39.610 --> 02:01:41.400
with so much beautiful information

2158
02:01:41.400 --> 02:01:43.380
and something you could probably display

2159
02:01:43.380 --> 02:01:44.410
on any coffee table.

2160
02:01:44.410 --> 02:01:46.370
It's really exquisitely rendered.

2161
02:01:46.370 --> 02:01:48.940
So congratulations on the book.

2162
02:01:48.940 --> 02:01:52.140
If people want to dive into your world,

2163
02:01:52.140 --> 02:01:55.880
literally and figuratively, beyond the book,

2164
02:01:55.880 --> 02:01:57.490
can they get involved with Mission Blue,

2165
02:01:57.490 --> 02:01:59.530
or like, where do you like to direct people

2166
02:01:59.530 --> 02:02:03.120
who wanna learn more about you and contribute?

2167
02:02:03.120 --> 02:02:06.190
Absolutely, get involved, support Mission Blue.

2168
02:02:06.190 --> 02:02:10.610
We are connected to more than 200 other organizations

2169
02:02:10.610 --> 02:02:12.773
that we partner with them,

2170
02:02:13.680 --> 02:02:16.930
find an organization that is local or national

2171
02:02:16.930 --> 02:02:21.180
or international that somehow strums with your heartstrings.

2172
02:02:21.180 --> 02:02:22.930
You say, "I like what they're doing

2173
02:02:22.930 --> 02:02:24.280
and let me sign up with them."

2174
02:02:24.280 --> 02:02:29.280
And for sure, National Geographic. I fell in love with...

2175
02:02:31.100 --> 02:02:32.530
National Geographic, when I was a kid,

2176
02:02:32.530 --> 02:02:35.830
was my library and my television, if you will,

2177
02:02:35.830 --> 02:02:40.306
before television existed, I could travel the world.

2178
02:02:40.306 --> 02:02:41.920
And it's still like that.

2179
02:02:41.920 --> 02:02:45.090
Were you the first explorer in residence?

2180
02:02:45.090 --> 02:02:46.060
I was number three.

2181
02:02:46.060 --> 02:02:48.090
Number three. Wow.

2182
02:02:48.090 --> 02:02:53.090
But now they've expanded that whole concept

2183
02:02:53.200 --> 02:02:58.200
and are supporting a wonderful network of young explorers

2184
02:02:58.400 --> 02:03:02.670
who are just at the beginning of their awakening

2185
02:03:02.670 --> 02:03:07.100
to what they can do in places all over the world.

2186
02:03:07.100 --> 02:03:11.530
And so it's really exciting to have been a part

2187
02:03:11.530 --> 02:03:14.775
of the Explorer program

2188
02:03:14.775 --> 02:03:19.775
that is in the DNA of National Geographic,

2189
02:03:20.640 --> 02:03:24.530
but to really have them focus on supporting individuals

2190
02:03:24.530 --> 02:03:29.530
the way they have come to be since, well, the late 1990s.

2191
02:03:29.870 --> 02:03:32.441
I came on board in 1998.

2192
02:03:32.441 --> 02:03:34.275
And they've tolerated me ever since.

2193
02:03:34.275 --> 02:03:35.570
(Rick laughs)

2194
02:03:35.570 --> 02:03:39.810
I think they've done a little bit more than that, Sylvia.

2195
02:03:39.810 --> 02:03:43.680
Well, come back and talk to me again sometime, will you?

2196
02:03:43.680 --> 02:03:46.055
Appreciate it. Thank you.

2197
02:03:46.055 --> 02:03:49.976
Peace.
Likewise.

2198
02:03:49.976 --> 02:03:52.559
(light music)

